\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Philippine stocks lead losses as inflation hits 6-month high\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2022\par 07:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSP\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterates stance on monetary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     * Thailand March CPI beats Reuters poll\par     * Yield curve still remains inverted \par \par     By Tejaswi Marthi\par     April 5 (Reuters) - Philippine stocks fell on Tuesday after\par the country's inflation rate exceeded market forecasts, hitting\par its highest level in six months, and the peso appreciated even\par as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintained that no policy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par needed for now.\par     Stocks in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Philippines\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gave up 0.7% and\par underperformed their emerging Asian peers, while the peso\par gained 0.4%.\par     The country's consumer price index (CPI) rose 4% in March,\par exceeding the 3.7% median forecast in a Reuters poll. Still,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BSP\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) said that monetary policy\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was not needed, and that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained\par cautious against possible second-round effects from supply-side\par pressures.\par     Inflation readings for March exceeded expectations as higher\par food prices exacerbated the effects of higher transportation and\par utilities costs, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said in a note. They said\par they expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSP\f28 \uc2 \f2 's first interest rate hike in the second\par half of fiscal 2022.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSP\f28 \uc2 \f2  governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Benjamin Diokno\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the surge in global oil\par prices could make average inflation breach the upper end of the\par bank's 2% to 4% target range this year, making it the second\par consecutive year of above-target inflation.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  also raised Philippines' fiscal 2022 inflation\par forecast to 4.1% from 3.7%, still lower than the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSP\f28 \uc2 \f2 's own 4.3%\par forecast.\par     Other stock markets in the region were mixed. Indonesian\par shares rose 0.1% and Singapore stocks advanced\par 0.3%, while the benchmark indices in Malaysia and South\par Korea fell slightly.\par     Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded largely flat against the U.S.\par dollar.\par     Oil prices jumped in early trading as uncertainty over\par supply loomed after talk of imposing a fresh round of sanctions\par against Russia, while Iran nuclear talks stalled.\par \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  also cut its growth forecast for East Asia,\par especially Thailand, to reflect the impact of the invasion of\par Ukraine by Russia, which calls its actions a "special\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ". The bank now expects Thailand's economy to grow 2.9%\par this year, down from a prediction of 3.9% in December.\par \par     Thailand's headline CPI for March jumped 5.73%, beating\par forecasts, driven by higher prices of goods and energy, the\par commerce ministry said on Tuesday.\par     Meanwhile, yields on \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes were little changed\par but the 2-year/10-year curve still remained inverted, hinting at\par a possible recession on the horizon.\par     Yields on Singapore's 10-year bond fell for a\par second straight day, by 2.3 basis points, to 2.311%, while\par Indonesia's benchmark bond yields rose to 6.759%.\par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:    \par     **  \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects 2022 growth of 5.0% in the developing\par East Asia and Pacific (EAP) region\par     ** Industrial stocks JG Summit and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SM Investment\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par , and Wilcon Depot among top losers\par     ** South Korea's March inflation accelerates to 4.1%, most\par since Dec 2011\par     \par   Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        \par  at 0422 GMT                                        \par  COUNTRY   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC          \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   INDE  STOCKS  STOCKS\par                       DAILY %  YTD %     X   DAILY   YTD %\par                                                  %  \par  Japan                  +0.20  -6.08  <.N2  0.05%   -3.61%\par                                       25>           \par  India                  +0.00  -1.61  <.NS   -0.24    3.78\par                                       EI>           \par  Indonesi               +0.02  -0.70  <.JK    0.15    8.28\par  a                                    SE>           \par  Malaysia               +0.00  -1.21  <.KL   -0.17    1.83\par                                       SE>           \par  Philippi               +0.31  -0.37  <.PS   -0.75   -0.19\par  nes                                  I>            \par  S.Korea   <KRW=KFTC    +0.20  -1.91  <.KS   -0.08   -7.45\par            >                          11>           \par  Singapor               +0.00  -0.55  <.ST    0.34    9.76\par  e                                    I>            \par  Thailand               +0.09  -0.24  <.SE    0.17    2.91\par                                       TI>           \par  \par  (Reporting by Tejaswi Marthi in Bengaluru; Editing by Kenneth\par Maxwell)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-5T