\par\par \b \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Cuts \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  to Neutral -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2015\par 12:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1011 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Nippon Steel & Sumitomo Metal\uc2  (5401.TO), Japan's steel making giant, slipped Friday on the back of a JPMorgan Securities Japan downgrade from Overweight to Neutral, citing a severe worsening of export conditions, including low steel prices overseas. House analyst Kazuhisa Mori recommends a wait-and-see approach while looking for signs of improvement in external conditions. He also cuts his target price from Y370 to Y350. Shares dropped 0.6% to Y309. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Aussie-dollar-bashing is the order of the day in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. New 2015 lows are being traded in AUD/USD, following the session's breach of $0.7537. Downside targets at $0.7502 and $0.7481 are now sought, and also $0.7306 in the slightly broader-term. AUD/JPY is at its lowest since late April, having breached Y92.68. AUD bears are targeting a 1.618 Fibonacci extension target at Y92.04, while longer-term bears seek Y90.58 on the monthly Ichimoku chart. AUD/USD is at $0.7540, while AUD/JPY is at Y92.70.\par \par \uc2 -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.\par \par \uc2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 0948 GMT With the Greek referendum upcoming, what matters for the rest of the eurozone is that contagion is limited, says David Tan, head of global rates at \uc2 JPMorgan Asset Management\uc2 . In particular, he says, depositors and investors must be reassured that the Greek experience cannot be repeated in other single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area countries. In his view, a 'no' vote is likely to bring \uc2 the ECB\uc2  pre-emptively into play. This won't be licence from \uc2 the ECB\uc2  for investors to take risks whatever the outcome but a reassurance that they will curtain negative outcomes in financial markets that may stop or reverse the eurozone's recovery. Mr. Tan reckons a 'Grexit' would also provide further impetus to fiscal integration among eurozone countries. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0948 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippines said Friday it is ready to argue its territorial claims against China in a section of the South China Sea. Lawyers for the Philippines will appear before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Permanent Court of Arbitration\f28 \uc2 \f2  in The Hague, Netherlands between July 7 to 13 to persuade the court to take jurisdiction over the case. China has refused to appear before the court and wants the Philippines and other claimants to pursue bilateral negotiations instead. If the tribunal decides that it has jurisdiction over the case, the Philippines will be asked at a future date to present its oral arguments on the merits of the case. (Cris.Larano@WSJ.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0943 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Energy think tank and consultancy firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2  has urged the Philippines to craft policy that will ensure a balanced power sector fuel mix, underscoring the risk of over reliance on a single fuel like coal. The Department of Energy is aiming for fuel mix of 30% coal, 30% natural gas, 30% renewable energy and the rest for other technologies. However, as matters stand, 42.5% of Philippine power capacity is coal fuelled--a number that could rise to 56% by 2020 as new power plans come on stream; natural gas will climb to 35% of capacity by then. "If coal projects are implemented as planned, Luzon's coal generation share will be over 75% by 2030, and many coal plants will be uneconomic," \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2  said, adding: "Without \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Philippines\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is on the path to having the highest coal share in Asia, despite DOE's intent to have the one-third rule." (Cris.Larano@WSJ.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0938 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India needs more reforms if it hopes to follow in the footsteps of more successful Asian economies, says research firm Capital Economics. To be sure, Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's government has made some noteworthy progress, such as shaking up the bureaucracy and lifting restrictions on foreign investment. But it has made little headway in implementing other crucial policy changes intended to ease labor and land laws and reform the tax regime. "Unless more is done in these areas we see little prospect of India emulating the likes of China, which managed to sustain growth of around 10% for a number of years." (anant.kala@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @anantvkala) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Eurozone retail sales in May rose 0.2% from April and were up 2.4% from May last year, beating consensus forecasts of a 0.1% monthly gain, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eurostat\f28 \uc2 \f2  data shows. Sales volumes in Germany jumped 4.2% from May last year and they rose 3.4% in Spain, indicating that household confidence is holding up despite the threat of Greece leaving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area. Retail sales data are notoriously volatile, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Christian Weil predicts a healthy pickup in private consumption growth this year. (nina.adam@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0922 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Despite signs that other areas of the economy are staggering, Britain's powerhouse services sector is showing welcome sings of a post-election bounce. The Services Purchasing Managers' Index, a key business survey released Fridayby Markit Economics, rose to 58.5 from a five-month low of 56.5 in May. A range of indicators suggest consumers in the U.K. were more cautious during the campaign for the general election on May 7 and ramped up spending once political uncertainty was left behind: British consumer confidence in June was the highest in the last decade and a half, market-research firm GfK Group said Tuesday. "Since real incomes are now growing strongly, credit conditions are improving and confidence is high, the U.K.'s economic recovery should maintain this strong pace over the rest of the year," said Samuel Tombs, analyst at Capital Economics. (jon.sindreu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0000 GMT (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--The bull failure high at 116.00 on the September gilt future is back in the immediate picture, following Thursday's impressive strength. Thursday's rejection at 114.41 produced a bullish piercing candle pattern on the daily chart, and the positive picture has been enhanced by further gains during Friday's current session. The 116.00 key high would be opened on a clean break above 115.75, while threatening additional upside scope to a minimum target at 116.35. The mid-June peak at 116.45 would also be opened. Intra-day support lies at 115.49, and only a push into new session lows below 115.33 would concern near-term bulls, exposing 114.84. The September gilt future is at 115.62.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Francis Bray, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analyst For Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0913 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Turkey's annual inflation rate may weaken further in July and August, to slightly below 7%, due to a further correction in food prices, says Selim Cakir, chief economist at TEB. CPI rose 7.20% from a year earlier, but dropped 0.51% from the previous month, the state statistics agency said Friday. "Still, the elevated core and services inflation suggests that once those favourable impacts dissipate, inflation is poised to turn north again. The weak lira, the reversal of oil price base effects and the stickiness of inflation should push inflation back up towards 8% by year end, in our view," he says. He adds that in turn, today's inflation print wouldn't lead to any change to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy stance. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0907 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] BMI Research says the finalization of Islamic banking guidelines in Malaysia will be a positive for the development of the industry and will help the country maintain its dominant position. These guidelines, which are a set of 11 standards, will complement existing guidelines and seek to address the current inconsistencies in the use of Islamic contracts. "As many standards are not legally enforceable, market practices vary across the industry, and the finalization of procedures will help to minimize discrepancies within the industry," BMI says. "While Malaysia's current Islamic banking standards are enforceable and have been in place for many years, they are technical instead of practical and are still open to interpretation." Malaysia is the world's biggest Islamic bonds market and the government projects the sector to grow at an average rate of 18% on-year in the next five years to $296 billion by 2019, compared with $141 billion in 2013. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jul 2015 06:11 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Cuts \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Neutral -- Market -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0903 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Citi says Fitch's unexpectedly positive outlook review for Malaysia is driving the benchmark KLCI index, quelling a major concern that has deterred foreign investment. Fitch revised its outlook on Malaysia to stable from negative and reaffirmed the country's long-term foreign and local sovereign credit rating. Fitch pointed at improving fiscal finances, something that will improve after implementing a goods and services tax and fuel-subsidy reform. The benchmark index closed 1.3% and 0.3% higher on Wednesday and Thursday, respectively on the back of the Fitch rating. The index is currently trading 0.2% down. Citi says it top picks in the Malaysian market include Public Bank (1295.KU), \f28 \uc2 \f2 IJM Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3336.KU), Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia Airports Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (5014.KU) and Top Glove (7113.KU). (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 03, 2015 06:11 ET (10:11 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150703eb73000ua\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
