\par\par \b \uc2 Southeast Asia's Markets Look to the \uc2 Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jake Maxwell Watts   \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2014\par 18:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  headed south in September, wiping out most of the year's gains, and the flow of money into local stocks became a trickle as investors prepared for U.S. interest rates to rise.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' swings have investors--some of them still smarting from a wave of selling that struck the region in the spring of 2013--wondering about the safety of holdings in local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Whether a slide by Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hammer other assets is "the right question to ask," said Jamie Grant, head of Asian fixed income at the Australian fund manager \uc2 First State Investments\uc2 , which manages $162 billion. "We've got one eye on [\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility] in case it's pointing to something in the future," he said.\par \par \uc2 But if there is a selloff on the way, it may bear little resemblance to last year's rush to the exits. Then, foreign investors beat a retreat, responding to hints from \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 , chairman of the U.S. Federal Reserve at the time, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would begin to scale back easy-money policies that had channelled cash into emerging markets. .\par \par \uc2 This year, several Southeast Asian stock markets have been among the world's top performers, thanks largely to reformist drives in countries such as Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines. Worries over shifting U.S. policies have come at a time when investors appear willing to assess each Southeast Asian market on its merits.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that expectations about what the \uc2 Fed\uc2  might do are pressuring regional markets. In September, the dollar surged 4.1% both against Malaysia's ringgit and against Indonesia's rupiah, outsized moves that, for many investors, were an uncomfortable reminder of last spring's selloff.\par \par \uc2 Stock-exchange data compiled by \uc2 Credit Suisse Group AG\uc2  shows that among Asian countries, only Thailand and India received net inflows of funds in September. That cut short an inpouring of cash that had been nearly uninterrupted since the start of the year, a shift that the bank called the "first signs of foreign investor capitulation" to pressure to leave emerging markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  swing more violently.\par \par \uc2 In the third week of September, the latest for which data is available, foreign investors withdrew a net $45 million from equities in Indonesia, $11 million from the Philippines and $51 million from Thailand, according to EPFR, which tracks flows of money.\par \par \uc2 In Southeast Asia, where rapid inflows and outflows of speculative funds often roil markets, policy makers are beginning to worry about the potential impact. Indonesian finance minister Chatib Basri said last month he expects that higher U.S. interest rates could send Indonesian markets into worse turmoil than that seen in the spring of 2013. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned that it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the peso moves too violently.\par \par \uc2 Still, share markets in Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines are up more than 20% in the year to date, trading as high as they were before last year's selloff.\par \par \uc2 Samir Mehta, a senior fund manager at \uc2 J O Hambro Capital Management Ltd\uc2 ., which manages $25 billion globally, says Southeast Asia has many strengths, pointing to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region have fared better against the greenback than the Australian dollar or Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 Domestic factors offset some of the global forces at play, economists say. Southeast Asian countries are stronger politically and economically than they were last year. A military-installed government has created some political stability for Thailand, and Joko Widodo, Indonesia's president-elect, is looking to introduce market-friendly economic overhauls.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's current-account deficit--a concern for bond investors--has improved. In the second quarter, the figure rose as a percentage of gross domestic product after two quarters at much lower levels, but declined in absolute terms to $9.1 billion from $10.1 billion a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised interest rates, making Indonesian assets more attractive. Mr. Widodo is expected to continue an effort by the current government to tackle subsidies that have added to the deficit by boosting demand for fuel.\par \par \uc2 In the Philippines, a stable government led by President \uc2 Benigno Aquino\uc2  and one of the fastest economic growth rates in Asia have won over investors.\par \par \uc2 First State Investments\uc2 ' Mr. Grant said that his investment-grade fixed-income fund will keep steady its holdings of Indonesian U.S. dollar bonds and has been adding to its rupiah-denominated holdings. The higher yields those securities offer relative to U.S. bonds are worth the risk, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Fears are overdone" about the potential impact of \uc2 Fed\uc2  moves, said \uc2 Pascal Blanqu\u233\'00\'E9\uc2 , deputy chief executive and group chief investment officer at Amundi, which manages around $1 trillion globally. The shifts are likely to create only short-term volatility in Southeast Asia, he said.\par \par \uc2 Instead of focusing on what the \uc2 Fed\uc2  might do, asset managers are looking more closely at developments within individual countries, Mr. Blanqu\u233\'00\'E9 said.\par \par \uc2 "Confidence is very much linked to the ability of the authorities to deliver on the fundamentals," he said. "Country risk is back in the emerging space."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jake Maxwell Watts at jake.watts@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2014 12:30 ET (16:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141002eaa2002jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
