\par\par \b \uc2 Taiwan Government Bonds Rebound; Banks Bargain Hunt -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2014\par 05:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan government bonds rebound after recent losses, with investor sentiment improving as early results from Scotland's independence referendum suggested support for the pro-union campaign, says a local trader. "Bargain-hunting from some local banks at major support levels helps lift bond prices," the trader adds. The most-traded 10-year bond yield falls to 1.7400%, off its earlier high at 1.7700%, versus 1.7500% late Thursday. The trader tips the yield to trade between 1.7200% and 1.7600% in near term. The five-year bond yield is at 1.2850%, off an earlier high of 1.3100%, versus 1.2870% Thursday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Although a large number of constituencies have yet to declare whether they have voted in favor or against Scottish independence, the results currently indicate a vote in favor of remaining in the union, says Simon Derrick, chief markets strategist at BNY Mellon in London. Sterling has risen back above $1.65. "Perhaps more telling, however, has been the move in euro against sterling, returning to levels that were familiar prior to the 2008 financial crisis. Given the continued political uncertainties facing the U.K., including tonight's result, this is a clear reflection of the ongoing pressures facing the eurozone," Mr. Derrick adds. EUR/GBP trades at 0.7830. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0347 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/INR has broken the daily Bollinger uptrend channel and could fall to the base of the daily Ichimoku Cloud at 60.35 as the rupee rallies on improving investor optimism. A daily close below the Cloud support could trigger more dollar-selling and thus an even stronger rupee. The India rupee rallied and stocks got a boost after China pledged $20 billion in investment to build industrial parks in two Indian states. The move has spurred speculative bets that large cross-border inflows will boost the rupee's value. The significant size of China's investment has helped USD/INR buck the trend of a broadly stronger U.S. dollar - triggered by changing expectations of how fast U.S. interest rates will rise. USD/INR is now 60.77 from its Thursday close of 60.73.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0344 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's expansionary 2015 budget plan could involve another policy rate cut by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  as early as next month, say ANZ economists. They add the budget will likely lift the country's growth by 0.2 percentage points to 3.9% next year. "The BOK will likely ease again in Q4 and may cut rates as soon as in October," ANZ says. It expects the BOK to cut the rate by 25 basis points to 2% and then stand pat through the fourth quarter of 2015. The rate has been kept steady this month following a 25 basis point cut in August to support the government's stimulus efforts. The government on Thursday said it will run a wider fiscal deficit in 2015 to spur growth. Government spending will grow by 5.7% to 376 trillion won ($363 billion) next year. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0333 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD pares earlier gains after hitting a near-five-month high on foreign investor buying, says a local trader. The pair is at 30.259, off its earlier high at 30.289, vs 30.256 late Thursday in Taipei. "Active buying by a few foreign banks pushed the USD/TWD to above 30.280 soon after the open, but there was not much follow-suit buying," the trader says. Rising local stocks and increasing exporter sales are weighing on the USD/TWD, he says, tipping the pair to trade between 30.240 and 30.280 for the session. The Taiex is up 0.2%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0308 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippines' financial market closure Friday due Typhoon Mario gives the peso respite from recent losses. The peso this week lost as much as 1.5% versus the U.S. dollar when it hit Thursday's 5-month low of 44.58. The decline was severe enough that it warranted verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amando Tetangco\f28 \uc2 \f2  of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a statement that the BSP is prepared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if peso volatility becomes excessive. This prompted traders to take profit on long-USD positions that were placed in reaction to the U.S. Federal Reserve's upgraded forecast for interest rates. USD/PHP ended Thursday at its day-low of 44.42 and may drop to the 44.00 round-figure trading barrier when it reopens Monday, hopefully in a more risk-positive climate after the Scottish independence vote. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0304 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The China yuan rises after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sets the yuan fixing stronger before the trading session kicks off. The yuan is at 6.1378 to a dollar, versus 6.1406 at Thursday's close. The central parity is set at 6.1455 versus 6.1490 Thursday. "The yuan's losses in earlier this week was just some smoke screen, it is set to rise more and it's now back to the right track," says a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY non-deliverable forwards are down to 6.2355/6.2405 from 6.2400/6.2420 late Thursday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0300 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nikkei\f28 \uc2 \f2  is up 1.4% at 16297.66 as the weakening of the yen boosts large caps, says Yusuke Sakai, senior stock trader at T&D Asset Management, which manages Y2.3 trillion in assets. If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nikkei\f28 \uc2 \f2  closes above 16291.31--the December high--that would be the highest since November 2007. "In terms of the real economy, I actually don't see anything making much progress. That's true for special economic zones or support for consumption. Government officials are thinking hard about how to pave the way for increasing sales tax to 10%. One of their means is stocks." Sakai says, though, the benefits of a weaker JPY is becoming smaller as negative effects grow, such as higher cost of goods imported. "Some are suffering," he says. "This is a difficult phase." Japan downgraded its overall assessment of the economy in September for the first time in five months. Speculation that Scotland will remain in the U.K. is also helping the risk-taking sentiment.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0256 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's regional banks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 DGB Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (139130.SE) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BS Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (138930.SE), which are focused on lending to small and medium-sized companies, will perform better than nationwide banks, says Citi analyst Joanne Lee. She adds SME lending represents about 73% of their respective loan portfolios versus the industry average of 43%. The government of South Korean President Park Geun-hye has been looking for ways to help SMEs, creating expectations of loan growth to the sector. In general, Korean banks' net interest margin in coming months will be negatively affected by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 25 basis point policy rate cut last week. \f28 \uc2 \f2 DGB Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  is up 0.9 at 17,300 won. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BS Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 0.6% at 17,400 won. The broader market is up 0.6%. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD fades from its six-month high of 1.2696 recorded on Thursday as the U.S. dollar index retreats on bets that Scotland will decline independence. Voting results are due later Friday. If the status quo is maintained in the United Kingdom, USD/SGD could slip to the 20-day Bollinger mid-support line at 1.2570--a technical barrier that proved to be an effective deterrent to dollar bears in early September. The U.S. dollar is likely to find support on dips due to the growing impression that the U.S. Federal Reserve may adopt a quicker pace of rate increases once its rate-hiking cycle begins. USD/SGD is now 1.2680 versus its Thursday close of 1.2663. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW rises to a new five-month peak, led by USD/JPY grinding to a fresh six-year high. The South Korean won continues to fall versus the U.S. dollar as punters, encouraged by the lack of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stall the won's slide, trade the USD/KRW pair higher. The recent spike in the U.S. dollar has prompted central banks in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Philippines\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Indonesia to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market to quell excessive fluctuations in their domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , say traders. Won traders are eyeing the 1,050 round-figure trading barrier that, if breached, could lead to another dollar-spike. However, the dollar is near the 1,046 top of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel--an indication that upside may be limited--which could motivate profit-taking ahead of the weekend. USD/KRW is now 1,045.8 versus its Thursday close of 1,043.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0225 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The U.S. dollar is likely to strengthen against the Malaysian ringgit in the rest of this year, UOB says. "As we approach the termination of the [U.S. Federal Reserve] quantitative easing by October, there are upside risks to USD/MYR, particularly as Malaysia's growth rate is expected to moderate in the second half of the year," the bank writes. The ringgit is among Asia's best performers against the U.S. dollar this year. Despite a recent stretch of dollar strengthening, the U.S. dollar is still weaker against the ringgit by 1.1% year-to-date. It trades 0.6% stronger against the ringgit early Friday, at 3.2428. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 18, 2014 23:52 ET (03:52 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140919ea9j0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
