\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Profit-taking weighs on Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , but set for weekly gains\b0\par\par\uc2 1,028 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2012\par 03:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Ringgit down on dlr-short covering\par \par \uc2  * Philippine peso eases on rate cut speculation\par \par \uc2  * Won turns higher on exporters \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, updates prices)\par \par \uc2  By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased on Friday as investors took profits after disappointing earnings from U.S. technology giants, although they were mainly poised for weekly gains amid signs the global economy is stabilising.\par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit lost 0.4 percent as interbank speculators covered dollar-short positions, while the Philippine peso slid on speculation of a rate cut by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week.\par \par \uc2  Regional stocks also eased as weaker quarterly results from \uc2 Google Inc\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  hit technology names in the area.\par \par \uc2  But the retreat in emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is simply a correction on profit-booking, dealers and analysts said.\par \par \uc2  Regional units are also expected to move higher against the yen if the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  takes another easing step at a policy meeting on Oct. 30, they added.\par \par \uc2  Most local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia have already gained versus the Japanese unit with the South Korean won hitting a six-month high.\par \par \uc2  "Still, U.S. funds are looking for attractive places for yields next year. We will see more inflows to emerging markets towards the end of this year, especially after the U.S. election and China's transition of power," said a European bank dealer in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  The dealer said those funds are likely to look to South Korean and Malaysian bonds.\par \par \uc2  Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were on course for weekly gains as economic data from the United States and China earlier this week alleviated concerns over a slowing global economy.\par \par \uc2  Some U.S.companies also delivered solid earnings, supporting risk appetite, although Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities such as central banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Singapore and Taiwan were spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2  The South Korean won led weekly gains among emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a 0.7 percent climb against the dollar. Offshore funds and domestic exporters chased the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar has risen 0.4 percent against the greenback so far this week, while the ringgit and the Chinese yuan have both gained 0.3 percent, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has advanced 0.2 percent, the data showed.\par \par \uc2  But the Indian rupee has lost 1.7 percent as higher-than-expected inflation data undermined hopes for a rate cut to boost the economy. Indian oil companies also bought the dollar to make payments.\par \par \uc2  RINGGIT\par \par \uc2  The ringgit earlier weakened past 3.0476 per dollar, the 38.2 percent retracement of its appreciation since Oct. 10 on dollar-short covering.\par \par \uc2  Technical charts indicated that the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may head to 3.0543, the 50.0 percent retracement, if it ends the day weaker than the 38.2 percent level.\par \par \uc2  However, some traders have been buying the ringgit on dips, limiting its depreciation.\par \par \uc2  "Besides \uc2 Google\uc2 , the market is in a risk-on mode. I will try to sell dollar/ringgit between 3.055 and 3.060," said a Malaysian bank dealer in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso slid as interbank speculators took profits from the second-best performing emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2  It also came under pressure from speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut interest rates at its policy meeting on Oct. 25.\par \par \uc2  Investors remained cautious over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, especially after it was spotted buying dollars below 41.20 earlier this week, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  "We will not tolerate excesses in exchange rate movements and will not hesitate to consider other tools in our policy tool kit," Governor \uc2 Amando Tetangco\uc2  said in an email.\par \par \uc2  Still, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  found some support at 41.45 per dollar on expectations of remittance inflows, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  "The peso can correct up to 41.57 and 41.75 if the BSP cuts in policy rates," said a European bank dealer in Manila, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "But for now 41.45 would be strong support, given that there is not enough negative news to reverse the recent risk rally."\par \par \uc2  WON\par \par \uc2  The won turned higher as exporters including shipbuilders and electronics companies bought the local unit to settle payments. Custodian banks also purchased the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2  The local unit started the day softer and weakened to 1,106.5 per dollar as some offshore funds and domestic interbank speculators sold.\par \par \uc2  But exporters did not miss the chances to buy the won on dips, especially around 1,106, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  "Given growing expectations for further appreciation in the won, exporters snapped it up whenever it weakened," said a South Korean bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2  Change on the day at 0700 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        79.30          79.28       -0.03\par  Sing dlr        1.2202         1.2189       -0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      29.245         29.269       +0.08\par  Korean won     1103.00        1104.30       +0.12\par  Baht             30.69          30.63       -0.21\par  Peso             41.40          41.32       -0.19\par  Rupiah         9590.00        9583.00       -0.07\par  Rupee            53.75          53.41       -0.63\par  Ringgit         3.0470         3.0345       -0.41\par  Yuan            6.2487         6.2503       +0.03\par  Change so far in 2012\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        79.30          76.92       -3.00\par  Sing dlr        1.2202         1.2969       +6.29\par  Taiwan dlr      29.245         30.290       +3.57\par  Korean won     1103.00        1151.80       +4.42\par  Baht             30.69          31.55       +2.80\par  Peso             41.40          43.84       +5.89\par  Rupiah         9590.00        9060.00       -5.53\par  Rupee            53.75          53.08       -1.25\par  Ringgit         3.0470         3.1685       +3.99\par  Yuan            6.2487         6.2940       +0.72\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Yena Park in SEOUL, Karen Lema in\par MANILA and IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing by Joseph\par Radford)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MAR