\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -BOJ gives another boost; won, Sing dlr at 4-1/2-mth high vs yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,022 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2012\par 04:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Philippine peso up on medium-term funds, macro accounts\par \par \uc2 * Won turns higher on BOJ easing; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caution grows\par \par \uc2 * Rupiah down on Indonesian corporate dollar demand\par \par \uc2 (Adds text, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Wednesday as Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined other major policymakers in launching fresh stimulus to shore up growth, bolstering risk sentiment and hopes for more capital inflows to the region.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won and the Singapore dollar\par \par \uc2 hit their highest levels versus the yen in about four and a half months after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BOJ) eased monetary policy by stepping up asset purchases by 10 trillion yen ($127.21 billion), double the usual amount.\par \par \uc2 The move invigorated emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most of which started the day slightly weaker against the dollar on worries about Spain's apparent reluctance to apply for financial aid. Asian shares also rebounded from initial losses.\par \par \uc2 "We got another excuse to get bullish here," said Andy Ji, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "There is already enough liquidity around and all it takes for those to come to the region is some feel-good stories," said Ji, picking up the won as the best choice for investors.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have appreciated so far this month as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  introduced a third round of quantitative easing and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a fresh bond-buying programme to ease the three-year old debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Still, Asian policymakers may not necessarily welcome those measures as they risk inviting hot money into their economies, and stronger regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to hurt export competitiveness at a time when global growth is faltering.\par \par \uc2 Business sentiment among Asia's top companies fell for the second straight quarter, dragged down by export-orientated economies such as China and Japan, while domestic spending helped boost Southeast Asia's outlook, according to the latest Thomson Reuters/INSEAD Asia Business Sentiment Survey published earlier.\par \par \uc2 Some authorities such as central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Taiwan have been recently spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. Investors remained wary of similar moves by central banks in the region.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also awaiting the latest data on Chinese manufacturing, with the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash purchasing managers' index (PMI) due on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso gained on demand from medium-term funds and macro accounts, and extended strength to hit a session high of 41.57 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the peso's gains were limited as domestic interbank speculators took the gain as chances to book profits, they added.\par \par \uc2 With traders seen still holding short dollar positions to cover, the peso is unlikely to extend gains here, a foreign bank dealer in Manila said.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won started local trade weaker against the dollar, but turned firmer after the BOJ eased policy.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 14.0644 versus the yen, its strongest since May 3.\par \par \uc2 The strength further boosted caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, especially as major South Korean companies such as \uc2 Hyundai Motor Co\par \par \uc2 compete with Japanese peers including \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  in overseas markets.\par \par \uc2 "Few will ignore the authorities and push up the won here, especially as yen/won collapsed," said a South Korean bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar was firmer than the previous close, which dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and exporters' demand for settlements.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying U.S. dollars to stem the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength not only in the main \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but also in the smaller market , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 In the prior session, the Taiwan dollar closed 0.2 percent weaker at 29.460 to the greenback, while it strengthened to 29.250 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in the market.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The rupiah lost 0.5 percent against the dollar on domestic corporate bids for the greenback and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not been spotted in the market.\par \par \uc2 Some state-run banks bought the rupiah, but dealers said Indonesian exporters were behind the demand, rather than the authorities.\par \par \uc2 While most other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefited from the BOJ's move, the rupiah did not much recover its losses.\par \par \uc2 "The rupiah will appreciate only when trade deficit narrows and growth numbers come out better," said a Jakarta-based dealer.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0650 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        79.07          78.81       -0.33\par  Sing dlr        1.2231         1.2255       +0.20\par  Taiwan dlr      29.323         29.460       +0.47\par  Korean won     1115.24        1118.30       +0.27\par  Baht             30.75          30.89       +0.46\par  Peso             41.59          41.75       +0.38\par  Rupiah         9540.00        9495.00       -0.47\par *Rupee            54.02          54.01       -0.02\par  Ringgit         3.0555         3.0610       +0.18\par  Yuan            6.3123         6.3189       +0.10\par  Change so far in 2012\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        79.07          76.92       -2.72\par  Sing dlr        1.2231         1.2969       +6.03\par  Taiwan dlr      29.323         30.290       +3.30\par  Korean won     1115.24        1151.80       +3.28\par  Baht             30.75          31.55       +2.60\par  Peso             41.59          43.84       +5.41\par  Rupiah         9540.00        9060.00       -5.03\par  Rupee            54.02          53.08       -1.74\par  Ringgit         3.0555         3.1685       +3.70\par  Yuan            6.3123         6.2940       -0.29\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Financial markets in India are closed for a holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ($1 = 78.6100 Japanese yen)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Ye-na Park in SEOUL, Miao-jung Lin in TAIPEI and IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing by)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \