\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Real Time Economics: Look Back: Broad Economic Concerns Mount World-Wide\b0\par\par\uc2 5,199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 22:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Real Time Economics blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and world economies are in danger again as financial leaders gather in Washington for the International Monetary Fund's annual meetings. Markets are falling world-wide due to broad economic concerns that go beyond a hangover from the crisis and extend to policy dysfunction in the U.S., Europe and developing nations. This post followed developments from the annual meetings and the latest readings on the economy for Thursday.\par \par \uc2 4:22 pm | by Jamila Trindle\par \par \uc2 The European debt crisis highlights the need for the Dodd-Frank law's changes to financial regulations, Commodity Futures Trading Commission Chairman Gary Gensler said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "Twenty-first century finance knows no true geographic border as money and risk can move around the globe with a touch of a button," Gensler said at a conference on regulation at Georgetown University.\par \par \uc2 Last year's Dodd-Frank law gave the CFTC the bulk of responsibility to oversee the over-the-counter derivatives market and the agency is still in the process of writing new rules for that market.\par \par \uc2 Gensler said that there are people who would like to roll back the rules, which would continue "the regulatory environment that led to the crisis three years ago."\par \par \uc2 Gensler also said that the CFTC is coordinating with foreign regulators to make the rules consistent and will seek public comment on the agency's jurisdiction over international transactions.\par \par \uc2 4:11 pm | by Paul Vieira\par \par \uc2 A coalition of advanced and emerging economies -- among them Britain, Canada and Indonesia -- sought Thursday to urge Group of 20 leaders to implement a coordinated strategy to rescue a weakening economy, according to a foreign-policy analyst who closely tracks the international forum.\par \par \uc2 A letter released Thursday from Britain, Canada, Mexico, Australia, Indonesia, and South Korea to French president Nicolas Sarkozy said the lack of visible political will in Europe over the summer, and the failure to find a lasting solution to sovereign debt problems, had damaged confidence and held back the recovery.\par \par \uc2 John Kirton, who heads up the University of Toronto's G20 research group, said the letter is an attempt by these countries to put together a deal. To their advantage, they are not linked to the big developed economies -- the United States and the euro zone -- or the so-called BRICS group of large emerging economies.\par \par \uc2 The joint letter also has an air of desperation, he added. "These leaders don't believe they can wait for Cannes," Mr. Kirton said, in reference to the G20 leaders summit scheduled for early November in the French city known for its film festival.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirton added that the euro zone, the United States, and the BRICS group -- China, Brazil, India, Russia and South Africa -- are at odds about what is required, highlighted by a tense encounter between Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner and his euro peers in Poland last Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirton added countries like Canada and Australia, which have relatively sterling balanced sheets, could agree to provide more fiscal stimulus, so long as the euro zone and the U.S. attack their large deficits and the BRICS come up with some form of financial assistance.\par \par \uc2 "But I am a bit pessimistic about such a deal," he said.\par \par \uc2 3:47 pm | by Anjali Cordeiro\par \par \uc2 Philippines Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima said Thursday that it is in the country's greater interest to focus on economic fundamentals and leave the level of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to markets.\par \par \uc2 His comments come as emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Philippine peso, are seeing big drops on rising risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 Purisima said at a Washington speech that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows markets to determine the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  mainly to smooth out any disruptive movements.\par \par \uc2 "Outside of that the best thing we can do is invest in fundamentals," he said.\par \par \uc2 His comments would suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy makers aren't inclined toward any dramatic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but would rather make smaller moves when needed to smooth out volatile swings.\par \par \uc2 "The best thing is really to leave it up to the markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 The government is keeping its budget deficit target at 3% of gross domestic product, he said, adding that the "official" estimate for 2011 growth has been 5%.\par \par \uc2 3:32 pm | by Phil Izzo\par \par \uc2 Nomura economists look at today's mostly flat jobless claims and sees no positive signs for employment. They write:\par \par \uc2 The overall picture of the labor market can best be described by the word "stagnant". Business confidence surveys have indicated that while businesses, at least outside of the financial industry, have not turned to conducting large-scale layoffs, they have suggested that a general lack of sales and uncertainty over the economic outlook are impeding hiring plans.\par \par \uc2 3:28 pm\par \par \uc2 Paul Krugman looks at the market tumble, and is in "I told you so" mode. He writes:\par \par \uc2 "When the 2008 crisis struck, it was immediately clear that this was what we had been afraid of. And it was desperately important that policy makers realize that we were in a world where the usual rules no longer applied.\par \par \uc2 But they didn't. The banks were rescued -- but as soon as that happened, the moralizers and deficit worriers, the people who see hyperinflation lurking under every bed, took over. Warnings that we were repeating not just the mistakes of Japan but the mistakes of Hoover and Bruening were waved away as the squeaking of people of no consequence, never mind the fact that some of us had pretty fancy credentials.\par \par \uc2 And now we are exactly where I feared we'd be, repeating all the old mistakes and experiencing all the old consequences."\par \par \uc2 Read the whole post here.\par \par \uc2 3:23 pm\par \par \uc2 So it is a puzzler why markets, having gotten the stimulus they expected, are now selling off. The sense of panic was such some analysts were already beginning to talk about whether the Fed would need to expand its balance sheet further to aid growth. The market unrest raises questions about how the Fed deals with markets, and whether monetary policy can really do anything for an economy where access to credit on affordable terms isn't the main thing holding the nation back.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the biggest problem for the Fed is the idea that in ratifying the market's expectations so closely over recent years, policymakers may now be in the position of chasing their tails. In both \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist and the recently ended bond buying program known as QE2, markets priced in the impact of the policies before they were actually implemented. It remains to be see what \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist's fate will be, but bond markets sold off after QE2 instead of rallied, as many would have expected.\par \par \uc2 Read the whole post here.\par \par \uc2 3:15 pm\par \par \uc2 MarketBeat is liveblogging the close with the DJIA currently down more than 450.\par \par \uc2 From the liveblog:\par \par \uc2 3:12 pm\par \par \uc2 Zero Hedge has a post up from Peter Tchir of TF Market Advisors, who is skeptical about the euro zone's recapitalization move reported by the FT:\par \par \uc2 "Europe is going to bail out the 16 weakest banks? Banks that are in trouble because the PSI for Greece is worse than any stress they ran. Plans should be getting made for these banks to die but to possibly save the creditors and limit systematic risk.\par \par \uc2 The focus should be on putting together plans for the big banks that pose real systematic risk. The market knows the stress tests were a farce, so why make a plan to save those that barely survived that low hurdle. Didn't they learn anything from dealing with Greece. Giving minimal help to the worst institutions is NOT going to solve anything and just shows how far behind they are in their thinking."\par \par \uc2 Read the whole post here.\par \par \uc2 3:00 pm | by Jean Yung\par \par \uc2 Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa must consider together raising resources for solving global issues, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said Thursday, reiterating a point raised in a joint statement by the emerging-market countries.\par \par \uc2 However, financial support from an individual country will vary depending on each country's situation, Zhou Xiaochuan said without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 The specifics of boosting support for the global recovery effort still requires wider discussion by the Group of 7 and Group of 20 nations, Zhou added.\par \par \uc2 He was speaking to media after a meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the BRICS.\par \par \uc2 2:45 pm | by Margit Feher\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign-bond purchases and liquidity-providing measures launched to prevent a contagion of deteriorating financial market conditions are temporary and do not replace the need for fiscal discipline, Vitor Constancio, the bank's vice president, said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The ECB remains committed to its primary mandate of maintaining price stability, he said. Using inflation to reduce debt would undermine the ECB's credibility, Constancio added.\par \par \uc2 "Inflating the debt away would unduly jeopardise the most precious capital of the ECB and of any independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , namely its credibility," Constancio said in a speech the ECB released on its website. "The ECB will never debase the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As our record shows, we have remarkably preserved both internal and external value of the euro by keeping inflation low and inflation expectations well-anchored."\par \par \uc2 2:39 pm\par \par \uc2 You can read the transcript from IMF chief Christine Lagarde's press conference here.\par \par \uc2 2:29 pm\par \par \uc2 The Financial Times is reporting that EU is looking to speed up bank recapitalization for the 16 banks at risk during the stress tests. From the FT:\par \par \uc2 "European officials look set to speed up plans to recapitalise the 16 banks that came close to failing last summer's pan-EU stress tests as part of a co-ordinated effort to reassure the markets about the strength of the 27-nation bloc's banking sector. [ 09-22-11 1636ET ]\par \par \uc2 A senior French official said the 16 banks regarded to be close to the threshold would now have to seek new funds immediately. Although there has been widespread speculation that French banks are seeking more capital, none is on the list. Other European officials said discussions were still under way."\par \par \uc2 Read the full article.\par \par \uc2 2:24 pm | by Phil Izzo\par \par \uc2 The Atlanta Fed says that despite the growing economic concerns, the probability of deflation in the U.S. remains low -- 16% as of yesterday:\par \par \uc2 "Prices of Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) with similar maturity dates can be used to measure probabilities of a net decline in the consumer price index over the five-year period starting in early 2011. One measure of this deflation probability was 16 percent on September 21, unchanged from a week earlier. This deflation probability has leveled off after increasing from 12 percent to 18 percent in the first three weeks of August. An alternative lower bound on the probability of deflation was 1 percent on September 21, also unchanged from a week earlier."\par \par \uc2 2:17 pm | by Jared A. Favole\par \par \uc2 President Barack Obama told European leaders that they need to take "forceful and decisive" action to deal with the euro zone debt crisis, the White House said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 President Obama met Wednesday with British Prime Minister David Cameron and French President Nicolas Sarkozy while at the United Nation's General Assembly. They discussed an array of foreign policy and economic issues.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary Jay Carney said Thursday President Obama said Europe needs to take "forceful and decisive" action to deal with the crisis. Carney, speaking to reporters aboard Air Force One while en route to Ohio with the president, said Obama also reiterated that he believes Europe has the capacity to deal with the debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 2:06 pm\par \par \uc2 European markets are closed. So what's the damage? From MarketBeat:\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 , down 246.80 points, or 4.67% to 5041.61.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Down 322.10 points, or 6.01%, over the last two trading days. \par  \par    -- Largest two day point & percentage decline since Monday, August 08, 2011. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stoxx Europe 600 , down 10.44 points, or 4.63%, to 214.89.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Down 14.21 points, or 6.20%, over the last two trading days. \par  \par    -- Largest two day point & percentage decline since Monday, September 05, \par       2011. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:53 pm | by Phil Izzo\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IHS Global Insight economists Nariman Behravesh and Sara Johnson say recession risks in the U.S. and euro zone are "uncomfortably" high amid disappointing economic data, political paralysis and the potential for a serious policy mistake. But they say this won't be a replay of 2008 financial crisis. They write:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While the risks of recessions in the United States and Europe have risen, a repeat of the financial meltdown of 2008 and the ensuing 'Great Recession' does not seem likely. Emerging markets will almost certainly continue to grow, albeit more slowly. Central banks stand ready to -- once again -- deploy unorthodox policies, if needed. That said, a financial shock emanating from the Eurozone could do a lot of damage."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:47 pm | by Nathalie Boschat and Gabriele Parussini\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone could look in the future at leveraging the resources of its bailout fund to increase its capacity to respond to systemic crises, French Finance Minister Francois Baroin said Thursday, although he cautioned this isn't a priority for now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone could in the future look at "how to give meaning to this fund by leveraging its resources to give it a systemic power," Baroin told reporters at the beginning of the annual meetings of the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Baroin insisted, however, that the immediate priority is for euro-zone countries to get a July 21 accord through national parliaments to stabilize the euro zone. The agreement will expand the capabilities of the bloc's rescue fund by allowing it to act in a preemptive manner, buy sovereign debt on the secondary markets and recapitalize ailing banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It wouldn't make sense to have an alternative strategy" until the July 21 agreement is in place, Baroin said. "We can't have a plan B until the plan A has been implemented."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:36 pm | by Jonathan Cheng\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From MarketBeat:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unlike other recent, ugly trading sessions that come to mind -- say, August 8's crazy 635-point Dow disaster, or Aug. 10's 520-point bloodletting, or even Aug. 4's 513-point slide -- this week's ugliness hasn't been punctuated by equally jarring moves to the upside. Nope, this week the stock markets have just kind of slid lower and lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For a sense of how gruesome things are, WSJ's helpful Markets Data Group reckons we're on pace for our worst stock-market week since October 2008, when we managed to lose 18% in five days. We're already below our August nadir, putting us at a one-year low. And gold, that heroic metal in times of ill times, is on track for its biggest dollar decline (-$81 so far) since 1980, and its worst in percentage terms (-4.5%) since Feb. 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stay tuned, folks. We still have, of course, about 10 hours to go in the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:30 pm | by Phil Izzo\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier this morning, the Conference Board release its index of leading indicators for August, which showed a 0.3% monthly gain. But economists Josh Shapiro at MFR Inc. warns that the underlying data aren't so encouraging. Stock prices and consumer expectations dropped and the positives came from two places that are artificially inflated. He writes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Abstracting from the yield curve slope component, which is influenced by the Fed's abnormally low federal-funds-rate target, and the recent jump in the M2 money supply component which is due to safe-haven inflows to the U.S. banking system from Europe as well as from money market funds, the overall signal from the LEI is in all likelihood misleadingly benign at the moment."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:25 pm | by Karen Talley\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Port of Long Beach, Calif., key way station for goods shipped in from Asia, is not expecting an overly merry holiday season.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Figures to be released next month are likely to show trade numbers for September were relatively flat vs year ago, as major retailers wait as long as possible to make decisions regarding their holiday season inventories, a spokeswoman said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retailers haven't ordered as many goods heading into the holiday season as they have in past years, also illustrating that there's still uncertainty about the overall economy, the spokeswoman said\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:22 pm | by Ian Talley\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European economic union is facing the threat of failure unless officials there quickly move to boost the firepower of its bailout facilities, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What Europe is trying to do is build the fiscal budget and financial complements to the initial Maastricht treaty," Geithner said. The Maastricht treaty is the euro-zone agreement that created the euro common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Without the broader fiscal and financial elements, their framework is not going to work," he said. "Europe's leaders recognize this is an existential threat to the endeavor of the euro," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the risk to the euro, Geithner said he's confident Europe will move ahead in the coming weeks to strengthen its backstop funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are going to see them act with more force in the coming weeks or months," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His comments come as world financial leaders and central bankers gather in Washington for the annual meetings of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank, and Group of 20 nations talks. Officials will focus on encouraging Europe to act swiftly and decisively to stem the crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and emerging markets have expressed frustration at the sluggish pace of efforts in Europe to respond to their crisis. European Union leaders agreed in July to make their bailout funds more flexible but have yet to ratify it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geithner said some countries have room economic space to encourage growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Some countries are going to need to move more quickly, and have no alternative," he said. "But many of the other economies have more room, more choice, to soften that path of consolidation so they're not hurting the economy as it recovers."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration for example, wants to enact a $447 billion stimulus package to boost employment. Economists say Germany can do more too.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geithner reiterated a call for China to pick up the pace of appreciating its yuan. A higher \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  value could boost global demand, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He spoke at a National Journal event.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:05 pm | by Arild Moen\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Immediate action is needed to restore confidence in political decision-making and to stop the euro-zone debt crisis from spreading beyond its borders, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council member Erkki Liikanen says in an interview to be published in Finnish magazine Suomen Kuvalehti Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also says it is crucial that changes to the euro-zone's rescue fund -- the European Financial Stability Facility -- are fully implemented and that it comes into force soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Right now there is a great risk that we won't be able to stop the European debt crisis from spreading into a global crisis," says Liikanen, who is also the Governor of the Bank of Finland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1:04 pm | by Laurence Norman and Ian Talley\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone needs to look at leveraging up its bailout fund once it has implemented agreed-upon reforms, European Union Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Olli Rehn said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on a panel ahead of the annual International Monetary Fund meetings in Washington, Rehn said he hoped to see current reforms to the region's bailout fund, the European Financial Stability Facility, completed by the second half of October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However he went on to say that it is "very important to look at the possibility of leveraging the EFSF resources and funding to have a stronger impact and make it more effective."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:57 pm | by Bob Davis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deputy governor of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday said that China would continue to buy European government debt, which he said could do "quite a bit at the margin to help" Europe handle its sovereign debt crisis. [ 09-22-11 1636ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking at a panel during the International Monetary Fund's annual meeting, Yi Gang said that finance ministers and central bankers gathered for the IMF meeting and also a session of the Group of 20 nations need to "discuss the world situation and come up with a feasible package that can increase confidence in the world." Yi didn't propose any specific solutions and other top officials say they doubt the weekend's meeting will produce any concrete outcome.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said that the chance of another global recession "is still rather limited."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:51 pm | by Ben Lefebvre\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's going on with oil? Oil prices have been swinging like a 1970s house party, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it knows why.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are trying to square resilient U.S. crude oil demand -- the Department of Energy yesterday reported a 7.3 million barrel draw in crude reserves last week amid increasing U.S. demand -- with a broader economic outlook that's still gloomy for Europe and US.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, demand for U.S.-made refined fuel is strong, helping the domestic refining industry. In all, the "U.S. oil market [is] facing same level of overall demand as it did prior to the 2008 financial crisis," Goldman says in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. benchmark crude recently down nearly $5.00 at $81.22/barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:38 pm\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Berkley economist Brad DeLong posts some dire charts with the headline:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another Slow-Moving Run on the Banks...\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:31 pm\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 I'm worried and scared - Mohamed El-Erian\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When I'm scared, that means a global recession is a certainty - we don't want to get there" - Mohamed El-Erian\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:29 pm\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Today's problems are largely economic, but the solutions are largely political" - Christine Lagarde\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:26 pm | by Corey Boles\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid all the economic turmoil, the last thing the U.S. needs right now is another fight that threatens to shutdown the U.S. government. House Speaker John Boehner promises that won't happen, despite the House defeat Wednesday night of a spending to bill to keep the government funded until mid-November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Boehner said that GOP leadership would gather with the Republican rank-and-file members to present them with options for changing the spending bill later Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But crafting a deal may not be easy. It is doubtful that the speaker will be able to lower the overall spending figure in the bill as that would then jeopardize its passage in the Senate, which must also approve it in order to avert a possible shut down of non-essential government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The other, and considerably simpler, option would be for Republicans to either increase the level of natural disaster relief funding in the bill, or to remove a measure stripping funding for a program boosting the development of cleaner-emission vehicles that is popular with Democrats. These were both reasons given by Democratic leadership for the fact that virtually every one of their members voted against the spending bill.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In doing so, they would likely win the support of enough Democrats in the House to pass the measure, but would run the risk of angering their own lawmakers even further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:20 pm | by Anjali Cordeiro\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More from Greenspan:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said Thursday that markets are signaling worries that something could "break" and it appears that Greece could default in coming weeks without a new tranche of money.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global financial markets and particularly riskier assets have fallen sharply on worries about global growth and the euro zone's growing debt problems. The market selloff worsened Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets "are fearful something will break and clearly Greece is weeks away from default unless there is a new tranche," Greenspan said speaking at a panel in Washington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, referring to the Federal Reserve's QEII bond buying program to boost the U.S. economy, Greenspan said it had one positive effect of weakening the dollar. But he also noted that the euro also declined, limiting the benefits of the weaker dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now, if the Federal Reserve wanted to, it could "unwind things with little economic consequence," he said. The Federal Reserve could do an "off market transaction" with the U.S. Treasury department acting as an intermediary, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ultimately if the Fed wanted to "they could unwind the whole thing with very little economic consequence," Greenspan said. The Fed could use certain "off market transactions" that have the U.S. Treasury department acting as an intermediary, Greenspan said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:18 pm | by Phil Izzo\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dropping consumer sentiment in Europe has some disturbing echoes, notes Francois Cabau of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 :\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Euro area September "flash" consumer confidence dropped to -18.9 from -16.5 in August, coming halfway between our and market expectations (BC: -20.0; consensus: -18.0). After last month's record 5.3 point plunge, today's 2.4 point drop is smaller but nevertheless significant. In fact, we find it interesting that last time the euro area consumer confidence number dropped by more than 5 points, it then declined by 2.1 points the following month, before falling 4.4 points the next. Those three months were October, November and December 2008."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:01 pm | by Matt Day\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Is copper trying to tell us something? Comex December copper was recently down 8% at $3.461/pound after earlier hitting the lowest level since August 2010. The contract has slid 18% this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Copper is telling you that the odds of a recession are increasing," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Adam Klopfenstein. "It's hard to paint a positive picture" for the industrial bellwether with the current slate of worries about the global economy, from slowing manufacturing in the euro zone and China to Europe's debt woes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodities in general have taken a hit today amid the increased economic concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:58 am | by Laurence Norman\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Olli Rehn disputed numbers the IMF is using to detail challenges facing Europe's banks. "It is essential that we continue to work in order to ensure the sufficient recapitalization of European banks. In this regard, we do not share the numbers of the IMF...but we share their same concern, and, I can just say that this is a work in progress," he said. The IMF has estimated that European banks have already lost 200 billion euros from the debt crisis and face potential losses of 300 billion euros. It has urged European authorities to ensure the quick recapitalization of the region's banks. Rehn, who was speaking at a question-and-answer session at the Peterson Institute in Washington, also dismissed concerns about Italy needing a bailout program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:54 am | by Ainsley Thomson\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro zone countries and institutions must act swiftly to resolve the euro crisis and all European economies must tackle their budget deficits to prevent contagion to the wider global economy, the leaders of six countries from the Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations said Thursday. In a joint letter, the U.K.'s Prime Minister David Cameron, Australia's Prime Minister Julia Gillard, Canada's Prime Minister Stephen Harper, Mexico's President Felipe Calderon, Indonesia's President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono, and South Korea's President Lee Myung-bak said the lack of visible political will over the summer, and the failure to find a lasting solution to sovereign debt problems, had damaged confidence and held back the recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The letter said euro-zone countries must look at "all possible options" to ensure long-term stability in the world's second largest international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:44 am\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former Fed chief Alan Greenspan weighs in on the turmoil, saying that markets are fearful that something will "break." He blames much of the crisis on Europe, saying that without Europe a U.S. double dip would be unlikely and noting that Greece appears weeks away from default without a new tranche of aid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:37 am | by Michael R. Crittenden and Nathalie Boschat\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 World Bank President Robert Zoellick, speaking earlier today, said the world remains in a "danger zone."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I still think a double-dip recession for the world's major economies is unlikely, but my confidence in that belief is being eroded daily," Zoellick said. He cautioned that the situation for emerging markets "may be changing for the worse," potentially threatening the global economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Falling exports were already a worry, now falling markets and declining confidence could prompt slippage in developing countries' investment and a possible pullback by their consumers too," he said. "A fall in developing countries' domestic demand would mean we'd lose their economic engines as drivers of global recovery."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:30 am | by Phil Izzo\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a sign of the flight from risk sparked by economic concerns, the yields on 10-year Treasurys have plummeted to under 1.8% - the lowest levels since the 1940s.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:23 am | by Thomas Coyle\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Employees the world over are feeling depressed, according to a survey by the consulting firm rogenSi. Job insecurity among workers this year is about where it was in 2009, when the global economy was still reeling from the U.S. financial crisis of 2008. Meanwhile 92% of those polled said their sense of self worth now stems from pleasing bosses, not intrinsic things like work ethic and self-belief. That's in keeping with the "culture of fear" that has held sway for businesses since the economy went rogue a few years ago, says rogenSI's Alex Jacobson.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:21 am | by Nathalie Boschat, Ian Talley and Gabriele Parussini\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Christine Lagarde, managing director of the International Monetary Fund, opened the annual meetings with a warning that world leaders need to take more decisive action to pull the global economy out of the current crisis. "There are signs of hope. It's just a question of having a little bit of patience and pushing leaders in the direction where they need to take more action than they have so far," the IMF chief said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In 2008 we managed to avoid protectionism and kickstart recovery. They don't have as much ammunition now," she said, referring to world economic leaders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:19 am | by Phil Izzo [ 09-22-11 1636ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The day began with varied worrying economic signals. The euro zone posted disappointing manufacturing numbers, giving rise to renewed fears of a double-dip recession. Economic bellwether \f28 \uc2 \f2 FedEx\f28 \uc2 \f2  echoed recent warnings that global trade has slowed and Asian shipments have been particularly weak. Meanwhile in the U.S., initial jobless claims fell a bit but remain at an elevated level that shows continued weakness in the labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-22-11 1636ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110922e79m0014u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
