\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Resume Climb, Prompting  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2011\par 11:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- An ebb in euro-zone worries, strong US data lift Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Central banks in South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore dollar climbs, resuming record highs after two months\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have snapped a two-month funk, testing recent highs and prompting some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from central banks Friday as investors put Europe's debt crisis behind them for the moment and as encouraging U.S. data spurred regional share gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks in South Korea, the Philippines and Taiwan apparently sold their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the resumed gains, local traders said. Most of the region's authorities had been sidelined since early May, when Greece's fiscal crisis flared up, sapping demand for risky assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the next bailout payment apparently headed toward Athens, risk appetite has perked up in recent days, boosting Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Strong U.S. job data Thursday helped propel them back toward their highs, with the Singapore dollar resuming its record run against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Craig Chan, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore, predicted the Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah will be among the biggest gainers in coming months, as global growth picks up again and the region's authorities allow more appreciation to counter rising inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia's central banks have tolerated significant gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the region has led the global economic recovery, but they still step in when they feel the rises are too abrupt or out of line with the trends of their trading partners.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's too early to say that we're at the end of the solvency issue for Greece," said Nomura's Chan. "But now there seems to be a little bit more clarity, and people are getting more hopeful that we're moving through the soft patch."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. private sector added 157,000 jobs in May, well above the forecast 95,000 increase, said payroll giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Automatic Data Processing Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., boosting expectations that Friday's closely watched nonfarm payrolls will also be strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With investors bidding up the Singapore dollar along with other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday, the U.S. dollar slid below its May 2 record low to S$1.2177. It had traded above $1.2500 several times in May as global risk aversion sent investors into the safe-haven greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the won climbed to its highest since the U.S. subprime crisis went global in August 2008, the Bank of Korea bought an estimated $200-$300 million, apparently in a bid to defend the KRW1,057.00 line after the dollar fell to the day's low of KRW1,056.20, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the BOK's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market team declined to comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seoul traders predicted the authorities will be less aggressive than in recent forays into the market, probably because a strong won helps to keep imported inflation from feeding the rapid rise in domestic consumer prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders in Manila said the Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to buy $50-$100 million, but the dollar still ended at a two-month low of PHP42.75. Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to buy the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around NT$28.760, Taipei traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine and Taiwan central banks couldn't immediately be reached for comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --By Martin Vaughan; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Min-Jeong Lee in Seoul, Phisanu Phromchanya in Bangkok and Fanny Liu and Aries Poon in Taipei contributed to this article). [ 08-07-11 0943GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110708e778000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
