\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Philippines Ayala Corp. CEO Sees Continuing Peso Appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 09:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 11 Mar 2011 03:50 EDT  WSJ: Philippines Ayala Corp. CEO Expects Inflation To Remain Manageable\par \par \uc2 11 Mar 2011 03:50 EDT  WSJ: \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ayala Corp. CEO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Slow Peso Rise Supports Remittance-Driven Spending\par \par \uc2 11 Mar 2011 03:50 EDT  WSJ: Philippines Ayala Corp. CEO Sees Continuing Peso Appreciation\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By James Hookway \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MANILA (Dow Jones)--Philippines-based conglomerate \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ayala Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the local peso \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue rising this year as mounting inflationary pressures compel the country's financial authorities to raise interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But its chief executive said the nation's expanding import bill could temper the peso's gains as oil prices continue to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jaime Augusto Zobel de Ayala, also the company's chairman, told The Wall Street Journal in an interview Thursday that surges of investment into emerging Asian markets over the past year or so "are generally considered a positive development in the region, but they sometimes have some unintended side-effects such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overvaluation, excessive monetary and credit expansion, or even unsustainable asset-price inflation," Mr. Zobel said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To cope with the appreciation of the Philippine peso, which has advanced 2.5% against the U.S. dollar this year and about 6.5% since the beginning of 2010, some of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ayala Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s export-driven businesses such as semiconductor firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Integrated Micro-Electronics Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., or IMI, and its business process outsourcing unit LiveIt Investments Ltd. use "simple hedges" which he didn't specify to make their revenue streams more predictable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, other Ayala businesses, such as its telecommunications business \f28 \uc2 \f2 Globe Telecom Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and utility provider \f28 \uc2 \f2 Manila Water Company Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., have benefited from a stronger peso, providing the parent company, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ayala Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., with a more balanced revenue stream. Both Globe and Manila Water have seen a stronger peso reduce the cost of their imports, such as components and equipment, Mr. Zobel said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, if it weren't for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the ascent of the peso in November last year, Mr. Zobel said the appreciation of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could have eroded demand among some of its consumer-related business, such as retail banking or shopping malls. That's because the Philippine economy is heavily dependent on the remittances that Filipino expatriates send home to support their families. Some 10 million Filipinos work abroad, including many in the United States, Europe and the Middle East, where their salaries convert into fewer pesos than before when they are sent back home.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Without the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy dollars towards the tail end of last year, Mr. Zobel says the peso could have strengthened by as much as 10% in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ayala Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . is one of the Philippines' oldest and most successful companies, operating real estate, banking and automobile businesses in addition to its telecoms, outsourcing and utilities businesses. In the coming year, Mr. Zobel said the company would be keen to bid for a series of new public-private partnerships being launched by Philippine President Benigno Aquino III's government. Many of the tenders involve contracts to build and operate major infrastructure projects such as toll-roads, railway lines and other, similar big-ticket ventures designed to upgrade the Philippines' economic potential.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Going forward, "we think there will continue to be appreciation pressures for the Philippine peso as rising global commodity prices could compel our local monetary authorities to tighten policy later this year. Our economic managers may also opt to use the exchange rate to anchor domestic inflation" by letting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate a bit more, Mr. Zobel said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "However, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation could be pared as the country's import bill could balloon amidst rising global oil prices just as geo-political risks threaten to soften remittances from overseas Filipinos this year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government officials say around 3 million Filipinos work in the Middle East, which has been unsettled this year by a wave of political upheavals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On inflation, Mr. Zobel said the situation was becoming "a bigger concern but does not seem to be as serious as the 2008 episode," when prices last spiked. He drew a distinction between the forces driving prices in his business environment, the Philippines, and other, more developed countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the U.S. and in most developed economies, higher oil prices tend to affect a bigger portion of the population as motor vehicle use is high," Mr. Zobel said. "In contrast, only a small portion of the population own motor vehicles in developing economies like the Philippines. So, while rising oil prices exert upward pressure on overall prices and tend to dampen consumption in the Philippines, food prices--particularly rice--tend to have a larger impact on the spending behavior of consumers."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ayala Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., rising inflation--the Philippines' consumer price index reached 4.3% on year in February, the highest rate in nine months--"could pressure the cost of credit to rise while softer consumer demand could soften our top line growth somewhat," Mr. Zobel said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By James Hookway, The Wall Street Journal; James.Hookway@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 11, 2011 03:50 ET (08:50 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110311e73b001y8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
