\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Won't Support Metals Much Longer -Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 08:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] Capital Economics says China's growing domestic production of ores, refined metals is undermining country's import demand; "given the current state of the market in China, it seems likely either that China's exports of metals such as copper will pick up, or its imports will tail off further, or both. Whichever happens, developments in China are unlikely to support world prices much longer," says Mark Williams, international economist at the consultancy. Adds, in contrast to base metals like copper, zinc, the steel market "looks relatively balanced." LME 3-month copper at $6,966/ton, down $14 with 1,354 lots done so far. (JAC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Risk for USD/SGD still "definitely on the downside" given USD/DXY has broken important levels, trader says; adds pair has plenty of scope to run down to 1.3700, where he expects is lower bound of MAS NEER band. According to people familiar with situation, MAS earlier today \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/SGD, buying at 1.3797. USD/SGD now 1.3787. Trader notes while Asian equities markets slightly weaker today, risk appetite still largely in neutral phase. "I think the trend is for more Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation near-term."(SRH)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD at 32.189, down on yesterday's 32.236 but almost unchanged from 32.190 midday, tracking weaker global USD, slight selling by exporters. Trading interest low given slow global markets for Thanksgiving holiday, says foreign bank trader. Investors also awaiting Taiwan's 3Q GDP data to be released around 0800 GMT. But even if data better than expected, "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the pair to fall under NT$32," says trader; tips pair in narrow 32.180-32.220 range in afternoon. (JEH)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 1.8% at 22,2216.15 vs 1.1% decline midday, as China markets fall more with Shanghai Composite down 3.4%, led by banks as fundraising concerns continue to weigh. Peter Lai at DBS tips index may test 21,200 medium-term if declines persist. "It's likely to see more downside in the near-term, as sentiment in China banks remains weak," says Lai. ICBC (1398.HK) down 2.8% at HK$6.61, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) down 3.5% at HK$6.90, Bank of China (3988.HK) down 1.8% at HK$4.40. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  (0023.HK) +3.4% at HK$34.80, after \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  upgrades stock to Buy from Sell. Volume strong at HK$46.40 billion, of which HK$4.85 billion coming from debutante Minsheng (1988.HK). (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market remains quiet with participants mostly sidelined after cashing out early gains. Overall volume anemic at over 733 million shares, with about 1.5 decliners for every gainer. STI down 0.9% at 2768.08, expected to end lower, with support at 2750. "There's not much business," says retail dealer at bank-backed brokerage; "business has already dwindled in recent weeks. It's even worse today." Despite sluggish broad market, some lower liners still attracting interest. \uc2 PEC Ltd\uc2 . (IX2.SG) +7.2% at S$0.67 after DBS Vickers starts coverage at Buy with S$0.88 target price; Swiber (AK3.SG) +3.5% at S$0.90 as company bags US$77 million contract to install gas pipelines in Myanmar; Cerebos Pacific (C20.SG) +4.8% at S$3.72 as company reports 26% on-year rise in net profit to S$25.1 million for fiscal 4Q ended September. (FKH)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds give up some gains on steady uptick in primary goods prices, 6.90% 2019 bond at 98.16 vs 98.21 before data, 98.00 last close. "Everything said and done, rising food prices are a fact of life, and the market was well aware of it before too," says trader with primary dealership. Adds, short-term sentiment still positive as market focuses on slowing federal bond supply. Tips bonds to find buying support at current levels. India's primary articles index rose 1.2% to 279.7 in week to Nov. 14 from 276.4 previous week. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets roiled after sharp USD losses overnight; USD/JPY briefly hits 14-year low at 86.29 as stop-loss sell orders around 87.00 triggered; decline comes despite ratcheted-up warning from Japanese Finance Minister Fuji that he will monitor USD/JPY movement "very closely." USD/JPY at 86.66 from 87.33 late NY overnight; EUR/JPY at 130.92 vs 132.25, dragged by USD/JPY. EUR/USD at 1.5104 vs 1.5143. USD's broad weakness drives regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, several central banks -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korea, Singapore, Thailand -- suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD. Regional stock markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.6% as exporters hit by strong JPY; S&P/ASX 200 down 0.3%, Kospi down 0.8%, Sensex down 0.3%, Taipei down 0.2%, STI down 0.8%, Shanghai Composite down 2.6%, HSI down 1.6%; Philippines +0.8%, NZX-50 +0.4%. Vietnamese shares down 4.1%, after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday announces VND devaluation, rate hikes effective December. Australian 3Q business investment down 3.9% Q/Q vs 0.2% fall expected. Singapore October manufacturing output growth weaker-than-expected due to continued weakness in pharmaceuticals; total manufacturing +3.6% Y/Y vs +6.3% expected. NZ business confidence tad down in November; NBNZ survey shows net 43.4% of businesses expect better conditions over next 12 months vs 48.2% in October. BOK widens its annual inflation target band to 2%-4% for three years starting 2010 vs 2.5%-3.5% range in 2007-2009. Philippine 3Q GDP +0.8% Y/Y vs +1.9% expected. Spot gold at $1,193.60/oz, up $1.50 from NY; hit record high of $1,195.10/oz early Asian hours, supported by EUR gains, news Sri Lanka's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys 10 tons of IMF gold. Gold helps LME 3-month copper hit 14-month high of $7,060/ton; last at $6,982/ton, up $2 vs London PM kerb. Nymex January crude down 60 cents at $77.36/bbl. (CNG)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian markets not yet reacting to growing controversy over bank bailout but it's worth keeping an eye out for negative surprises, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 ; "the market seems to have regarded the \uc2 Bank Century\uc2  case as 'noise' though we think it potentially could have adverse consequences." Allegations that part of $700 million bailout funds were misused during rescue of \uc2 Bank Century\uc2  in 2008 have prompted an investigation; roles of Vice President Boediono, Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati under question. Bailout controversy, alongside ongoing furore over separate scandal in which officials trying to shut down country's antigraft commission, has drawn some public criticism of President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono's government; \uc2 Bank Century\uc2  case "could also delay economic initiatives and reforms as key policymakers may be distracted," Citi says. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR holds head over water even as greenback falls to 14-year low against JPY while dollar index (USD vs 6 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) hits 15-month low; demand for pair mainly driven by oil importers who settle import bills at end of every month, says dealer with state-run bank. "Today the market is slightly delinked from global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks. Most of the current strength in the dollar is due to importer demand," says dealer. Pair at 46.27 after touching 46.12 intraday low, 46.32 intraday high vs 46.21 late Wednesday, dealer says bidding for pair likely to outweigh selling pressure for remaining sessions in November, following which INR should show resurgence. Sensex down 0.3% at 17,149.01. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares fall further later in afternoon session, led by banks; "today's decline has been steeper than expected, there's a heightened fear of banks' fundraising plans and the specter of possible monetary tightening," says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. Says today's sharp loss "doesn't bode well for the market outlook at all," if banks, which have 30% weighting in Shanghai index, continue to fall, then difficult to give support level for index rest of year. Shanghai Composite down 3.1% at 3189.52, tipped to hold around 3200 rest of session. \uc2 China Minsheng Banking Corp\uc2 . (600016.SH) off 5.5% at CNY7.90, Bank of China (601988.SH) down 4.0% at CNY4.11. Shenzhen Index down 2.7% at 1181.02. (NYW)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] HKD IRS flat, short-ends remain at record lows, as rock-bottom U.S. rates likely to persist for longer period, unlikely to see any meaningful rise in near-term; longer-ends relatively better supported, as Fed tipped to hike rate late 2010/early 2011, trader says. Trading to remain thin, as corporations have no need to hedge interest rate risks. HKD 1-year IRS 0.32% vs 0.31% late yesterday, 2-year flat at 0.88%. 5-year steady at 2.23%, 10-year 3.00% vs 3.01%. (ARP)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says weaker-than-expected 3Q GDP, downward revision in 2Q growth put its full-year 1.6% growth forecast for Philippines at risk of being missed; however, house remains hopeful still-loose monetary policy, strong overseas workers remittance will provide strong last quarter to GDP; says 4Q growth usually picks up since the "Filipino consumer switches out of a precautionary savings mode, especially during the Christmas season." (SFL) [ 11-26-09 0201ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091126e5bq00053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
