\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-UPDATE 1-Philippine peso's rally on rate hike may fade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2008\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with Standard Chartered forecast)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 18 (Reuters) - The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hefty rate rise and its hawkish stance will bolster the ailing peso in the short term, but more tightening may hurt the economy and undermine the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the longer run, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Also, concerns about a fallout from lofty global oil and food prices on the economy may not recede quickly though the market expects further interest rate rises to help restrain prices.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts raised their outlook on the peso following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive 50-basis-point rate hike on Thursday and hints of further tightening, but most have maintained their forecasts on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 UBS\uc2  said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates by a total 200 basis points in the coming months to fight inflation, which hit a 14-year high of 11.4 percent in June.\par \par \uc2 That included the latest rate rise of 50 basis points and an earlier increase of 25 basis points in June.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, \uc2 UBS\uc2  has raised its one-month and three-month peso forecasts to 44.50 per dollar each -- little changed from the current level, analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Definitely, the 50-basis-point rate hike gives a more positive tone to the peso in the near term," said Callum Henderson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 "But over the longer term, the more they tighten now, the greater the chance of further growth deceleration and further peso weakness in the first half of 2009," he said.\par \par \uc2 Standard Charted Bank has raised its peso forecast to 44.75 per dollar by the end of September from 45.50 and raised its end-2008 outlook to 45 from 46, analysts at the bank said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=>, among Asia's worst performers this year, jumped 1 percent to a 3-week high of 44.44 per dollar in onshore trade, buoyed by the rate rise and falling oil prices.\par \par \uc2 It has lost 7.3 percent against the dollar so far this year, after it gained 19 percent as the region's top performer in 2007.\par \par \uc2 The peso rose as high high as 44.41 per dollar in one-month offshore non-deliverable forwards <PHP1MNDFOR=>, a one-month high, and hit 44.65 per dollar in three-month NDFs <PHP3MNDFOR=>, its strongest level since early June.\par \par \uc2 OUTLOOK UNCERTAIN The Philippine economy expanded a robust 7.2 percent last year, but a global economic slowdown and soaring costs have cast a pall over this year's prospects.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll this month forecast annual economic growth to slow to 5.1 percent this year and ease to 5.0 percent in 2009.\par \par \uc2 The market had been dumping the peso in recent weeks as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen heavily exposed to high oil prices, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  imports nearly all of its crude oil needs and is the world's biggest rice importer. Its trade deficit this year is set to expand by a third to about $11 billion, the highest in at least nine years, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  documents show.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices <CLc1> bounced above $130 a barrel on Friday, having risen almost 37 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Grappling with slowing growth but more concerned about inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point in June. Before that, it cut interest rates by a total of 250 basis points since July 2007.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates may help limit the peso's further weakness as the cental bank appears determined to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help contain imported inflation, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, the technical environment appears conducive for a near-term dollar/peso reversal. Outstanding positions appear net dollar long, carry attraction to short dollar/peso is high, rates are higher and the BSP (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has been capping spot upside," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "That said, a more sustained spot descent will likely be contingent on further policy follow-through on the hawkish BSP rhetoric," he added.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at DBS Bank maintain their year-end forecast at 47 per dollar, citing persistent inflationary risks.\par \par \uc2 "With BSP looking for double-digit inflation to persist in the first quarter of 2009, the real policy rates in deep negative, the stock market and bond market will not be in a hurry to rally," they said in a note.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PHILIPPINES/PESO (ANALYSIS, UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|PHD|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080721e47i000l9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
