\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Philippines \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to support weak peso\b0\par\par\uc2 140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2008\par 03:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 4 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday by selling dollars to support the struggling peso, traders said, after the country's annual inflation rose to a 14-year high in June.\par \par \uc2  The peso <PHP=> fell to 45.7 against the dollar, its weakest in more than nine months, as concerns over rising food and oil prices weighed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling dollars today at 45.70 pesos per dollar, and I think they may signal higher interest rates again later this month," a Manila-based trader said.\par \par \uc2  The peso has fallen 9.7 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2  (Reporting by Melissa Chia; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PHILIPPINES/PESO (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|PHD|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080704e4740002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
