\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia trades narrowly, supported by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Chia   \par \uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2008\par 05:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 1 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded narrowly on Tuesday as investors warily held back following decade-high inflation rates in Thailand and South Korea, while authorities were suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in Indonesia, Malaysia, India and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  were suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South Korea's annual inflation in June rose to 5.5 percent from 4.9 percent in May, hitting its highest in almost 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Subsequently, the Korean won <KRW=> weakened to 1,048.75 per dollar, 0.3 percent lower than its previous close of 1,046 <KRW=KFTC>, while market players kept a look-out for possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier on, several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region were buoyed by a pullback in oil prices <CLc1>, which hit a record high above $143 per barrel on Monday, but these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave up gains as oil prices rebounded to around $141 per barrel on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> inched up by 0.1 percent to 9,215 against the dollar and held around those levels throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Jakarta said the authorities had sold dollars everyday for the past few weeks to support the rupiah, and were doing the same on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The government cannot subsidise our local oil, and this has caused higher inflation, so another way to (support growth) is to maintain the dollar/rupiah pairing at a cozy rate so our industries can run well," an Indonesia-based trader said.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's annual inflation rate rose to a 21-month high of 11.03 percent in June, due largely to the lifting of fuel subsidies in the country.\par \par \uc2 The country's policy setting meeting will take place on Thursday, and Robert Prior-Wandesforde at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said he expected a 25-basis-point hike in the present interest rate of 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We are expecting another quarter-point move on Thursday and a total of 125 basis points of hikes by the end of this year, taking the policy rate up to 9.75 percent," the economist said.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's consumer prices in June rose 8.9 percent from a year earlier, higher than the expected 8.5 percent polled in a Reuters survey. However, markets in Thailand were closed for a mid-year holiday.\par \par \uc2 MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITED\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> fell to a 15-month low of 43.437 on heightened concerns over the impact of rising oil on the economy's growth. Traders said they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been selling dollars early in the day.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Manila also cited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso <PHP=>, which hit a 9-month low of 45.02.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protecting the peso around 44.99 to 45 per dollar," a trader said.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> steadied around its previous closing levels of 3.265 per dollar, and traders said they suspected the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold dollars to support the ringgit at 3.267-per-dollar throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 "Seems foreigners are not willing to touch this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term until political news is gone," a trader in Kuala Lumpur said.\par \par \uc2 Leader of Malaysia's revitalised opposition, Anwar Ibrahim, recently sought refuge at the Turkish embassy following sodomy accusations. The former deputy premier fled to the embassy fearing for his security. [ID:nKLR151860]\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> peaked briefly at a 2-week high of 30.294, buoyed by the U.S. dollar which fell against the yen <JPY=> after the Bank of Japan tankan survey showed a smaller-than-expected decline in Japanese business sentiment. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0759 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       105.80         105.52       -0.26\par  Sing dlr        1.3623         1.3600       -0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      30.353         30.354       +0.00\par  Korean won     1046.80        1046.00       -0.08\par  Peso             45.00          44.90       -0.22\par  Rupiah         9215.00        9225.00       +0.11\par  Rupee            43.35          43.02       -0.76\par  Ringgit         3.2640         3.2650       +0.03\par  Yuan            6.8579         6.8543       -0.05\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       105.80         111.33       +5.23\par  Sing dlr        1.3623         1.4382       +5.57\par  Taiwan dlr      30.353         32.443       +6.89\par  Korean won     1046.80         935.70      -10.61\par  Baht             33.49          33.68       +0.57\par  Peso             44.45          41.28       -