\par\par \b \uc2 TABLE-Asia ex-China's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves fall $9 bln in May\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2008\par 12:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 10 (Reuters) - Asian holdings of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, excluding those of China's, fell for the second staight month, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed, as some central banks sold dollars to support their weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Asia's reserves, excluding China's, in May fell by nearly $9 billion from April to $2.54 trillion, following a $10 billion decline in April from March, the data showed.\par \par \uc2  The overall decrease was led by an $6.86 billion dip in Japan's reserves, the second straight monthly decline, as higher U.S. yields cut the value of bonds in Japan's reserves while the euro's fall versus the dollar also dented the stockpile.\par \par \uc2  South Korea's reserves fell by $2.28 billion, possibly due to the dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities to prop up the won <KRW=>.\par \par \uc2  Other central banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India, Thailand and Indonesia have also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in recent weeks to limit the declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of concern that further weakess could stoke inflationary risks.\par \par \uc2  That marks a shift from Asian central banks' traditional dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their exchange rates competitive, resulting in the rapid build-up of their reserves.\par \par \uc2  China releases reserves data at the end of every quarter. Its holdings, the world's largest, totalled $1.68 trillion at the end of March. Latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Country          Reserves    As at     end-2007    percentage\par              $ billion            $ billion       change\par  China          1682.20    Mar 31      1528.20       10.08\par  Japan           996.98    May 31       973.37        2.43\par  Taiwan          290.07    May 31       270.31        7.31\par  South Korea     258.20    May 31       262.22       -1.53\par  India           314.61    May 30       275.56       14.17\par  Singapore       175.80    May 31       162.95        7.89\par  Hong Kong       159.00    May 31       152.70        4.13\par  Malaysia        125.20    May 30       101.30       23.59\par  Thailand        108.90    May 30        87.60       24.32\par  Indonesia        57.50    May 30        57.04        0.81\par  Philippines      36.60    May 31        33.75        8.44\par  Pakistan         11.18    May 31        15.74      -28.97\par  Bangladesh        5.33    May 31         5.51       -3.27\par  TOTAL         4,221.57               3,926.25        7.52 Asia\par ex-China  2,539.37\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * In April 2005, China injected $15 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves into the Industrial and Commercial Bank of\par China  , the country's largest lender, to\par help it restructure to sell stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * In December 2003, China used $45 billion of reserves to\par bail out \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par [CCB.UL] .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Those funds are not included in this figure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Sources: central banks.\par  (Reporting by Kevin Yao, Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ASIA-RESERVES (TABLE)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|J|SNI|SNS|PHD|SI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080610e46a000qj\par }\