\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia rebounds on Fed move, but caution remains   \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2007\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 20 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced back on Monday as regional stocks rallied after the U.S. Federal Reserve cut its discount rate in a surprise move on Friday, but the spectre of a credit market squeeze still weighed on sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 The Korean won <KRW=> steadied near 943 per dollar after hitting 939.9, which was up about 1 percent from late Asian trade on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The high-yielding Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> rose as far as 9,380 per dollar, up nearly 1 percent, although it remains the worst performer in Asia this year with a loss of 4.2 percent versus the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 A Jakarta-based trader doubted that the rupiah would sustain its rally in the near term, given continuuing jitters about the U.S. subprime market crisis.   \par \par \uc2 "The subprime issue is still not finished -- there is still some doubt about it. If the subprime woes turn up again, I'm afraid the rupiah will go down again," he said.   \par \par \uc2 But Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would want to stop the rupiah falling below the 9,500 level, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Last week, central banks in Indonesia, Malaysia and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  were reported to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors fled risk amid credit market turmoil.   \par \par \uc2 MSCI's measure of Asia Pacific stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> jumped 5.5 percent following a rebound in U.S. stocks on Friday after the Fed cut its discount rate.   \par \par \uc2 Leading the pack, Indonesian stocks <.JKSE> jumped 6.6 percent, while South Korean shares <.SK11> surged 5.7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 NOT OUT OF THE WOODS   \par \par \uc2 Rising local stocks helped drive the Singapore dollar <SGD=> as far as 1.5240 per U.S. dollar, up almost 0.9 percent from late Asian trade on Friday and its strongest in nearly a week.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Thai baht <THB=THB> rose as high as 34.30 per dollar, up more than 0.8 percent, while the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> gained about 0.6 percent to 3.491 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Philippine markets were closed for a public holiday.   \par \par \uc2 Many analysts remained cautious as fears of a global credit squeeze persisted, even though the Fed's statement accompanying its discount rate cut fuelled expectations that it could be moving towards lowering its benchmark fed funds rate target.   \par \par \uc2 Thomas Harr, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank, said he would refrain from telling his clients to buy into Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now, despite the recovery in Asian stocks.   \par \par \uc2 "I think what we are seeing today is obviously a little bit of recovery, which is natural given that the Fed at least did something by cutting the discount rate on Friday," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "But the feeling in the market is that this may not improve liquidity that much. It's not clear that we are out of the woods yet and we expect the market to be choppy in the coming days." The Japanese yen, which has moved in the opposite direction to most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions as investors unwound carry trades, eased against the dollar <JPY=> after pulling back from a 14-month high on Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0729 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   