\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asian high-yielders buckle under credit fears   \b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2007\par 05:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 15 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso, Indonesian rupiah and Singapore dollar hit multi-week lows on Wednesday as investors extended their aggressive sales of risky assets on fears U.S. credit market woes were spreading overseas.   \par \par \uc2 The high-yielding peso <PHP=> fell 0.8 percent to 46.27 per dollar, a five-week low, while the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>, another high-yielder, fell to 9,412 per dollar, its weakest point since June 2006.   \par \par \uc2 Traders in Jakarta said Bank Indonesia must have sold between $800 million and $900 million as it tried to brake the rupiah's decline and stop it from slipping beyond 9,400 per dollar. Central banks in Malaysia and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=>, sterling <GBP=> and Australian and New Zealand dollars fell as investors moved out of higher-yielding assets and bought back the low-yielding yen <JPY=>. The yen hit a 4-1/2-month high against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed market participants were pushing back the possibility of a rise in official Japanese rates to September from August. The euro has also been hurt by a paring of the chances of a rate rise by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next month.   \par \par \uc2 "Indonesia will, of course, be an issue when you have this kind of risk aversion because of the high yields," said UOB Bank strategist Jimmy Koh.   \par \par \uc2 Koh said he was more worried about other asset markets than about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "So far it has been an issue of market sentiment. But it can become a fundamental issue if there is a sharp pullback in liquidity and that introduces a risk premium into other sectors of the economy, thereby dampening consumption," Koh said.   \par \par \uc2 POSITIONS UNWOUND   \par \par \uc2 Markets in South Korea and India were closed for holidays. The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell more than half a percent to the weaker side of 1.53 per U.S. dollar. The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> hit 3.4930 per dollar, its weakest since the end of June. "Currently, we're having a bit more pressure on the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result of base dollar strength, not so much against the yen but more against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Thio Chin Loo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 BNP Paribas.\par \par \uc2 "It's just a symptom of unwinding of risk across the board and this has affected Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well," she added.   \par \par \uc2 The flow of bad news from credit markets has not abated.   \par \par \uc2 Concern grew after a U.S. investment firm wanted to halt redemptions on Tuesday and a Canadian trust couldn't find funds to repay some short-term debt.   \par \par \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  , the world's largest retailer, cut its profit forecast and Swiss Bank  \uc2 UBS\uc2  warned that market turmoil was likely to hit its investment banking business.   \par \par \uc2 "We continue to see price action driven primarily by crisis management and position liquidation rather than active decision making," said  \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  strategist Christy Tan.   \par \par \uc2 "We're still in the midst of a deleveraging environment," she said.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0800 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 C