\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupiah leads weak Asia on equity falls   \b0\par\par\uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2007\par 03:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 28 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah led the decline in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday as regional stock markets fell and investors unwound yen-funded carry trades following a steep sell-off in global stock markets.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Indonesian and Philippine central banks sold the dollar for their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the rupiah and peso led a broad decline in most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The rupiah <IDR=> recovered from a three-month low of 9,195 per dollar, but was still down about 0.8 percent at 9,150 per dollar. The peso <PHP=> was near 48.50 per dollar, down a quarter of a percent and just off three-week lows.   \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> fell a third of a percent to 941.6 per dollar, while the Singapore dollar <SGD=> also dipped nearly 0.3 percent to around 1.53 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts remained bullish about the underlying strength of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general, citing the region's solid economic growth and hefty balance-of-payment surpluses.   \par \par \uc2 "I think what we see right now in the market is just a reaction to what happened yesterday, so you see people starting to get a bit risk averse again," said Magnus Prim, chief Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SEB.   \par \par \uc2 "But I see the fundamentals are still pretty good and the falls should prove to be a short-term correction," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> fell against the dollar, taking a breather after surging to 10-week highs in the previous session as investors unwound risky trades on fears of rising volatility.   \par \par \uc2 A tumble in global stock prices including the biggest daily fall in Chinese stocks in a decade, soft U.S. economic data and growing tensions with Iran jolted investors from complacency and prompted them to cut exposure to risk.   \par \par \uc2 The Chicago Board Options Exchange Volatility Index, or VIX <.VIX>, rose 70 percent on Tuesday in another sign of investor jitters. The VIX typically rises as investors become anxious about the stock market and tends to fall when fear subsides.   \par \par \uc2 Asian equity markets <.MSCIAPJ> were weaker on Wednesday, but Chinese stocks <.SSEC> rose about 4 percent on Wednesday after falling nearly 9 percent on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> hit a new intraday post-revaluation high of 7.7378 per dollar, extending the previous day's gains.   \par \par \uc2 RUPIAH, PESO FACE BIGGER PRESSURE   \par \par \uc2 Aslim Tadjuddin, a deputy Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, said that the bank views a sharp fall in the rupiah as temporary and will do "everything necessary" to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 But traders said the central banks would not over-react.   \par \par \uc2 "They just want to smooth the movement, nothing more than that," said a trader in Singapore, referring to the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indonesia and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan analysts Claudio Piron and Yen Ping Ho said in a research note that the rupiah, the peso and the Indian rupee would face greater pressure as investors had borrowed the cheaper yen to build long positions in the units.   \par \par \uc2 "While we struggle to identify fundamental changes in the external backdrop that warrants a fundamental reversal of the carry backdrop, we acknowledge that the technical environment of market caution, heavy long risky asset positions and tight valuations mean pressures could remain for some time."   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht, which has been caught in a two-tier market since the government imposed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in December, gained about 0.3 percent to 35.43 per dollar onshore <THB=>.   \par \par \uc2 But it was volatile in offshore trading <THB=W>, moving between 33.05 and 34.10 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand on Wednesday cut its benchmark 1-day repurchase rate by 25 basis point to 4.5 percent, as expected. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0742 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.59         119.43       +0.71\par  Sing dlr        1.5287         1.5249       -0.25\par  Taiwan dlr      32.946         32.950       +0.01\par  Korean won      941.60         938.60       -0.32\par  Baht             35.43          35.53       +0.28\par  Peso             48.50          48.38       -0.25\par  Rupiah         9150.00        9080.00       -0.77\par  Rupee            44.28          44.21       -0.15\par  Ringgit         3.5015         3.4945       -0.20\par  Yuan            7.7386         7.7415       +0.04\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.59         119.01       +0.35\par  Sing dlr        1.5287         1.5337       +0.33\par  Taiwan dlr      32.946         32.585       -1.10\par  Korean won      941.60        0929.50       -1.29\par  Baht             34.10          36.08       +5.81\par  Peso             48.50          49.03       +1.09\par  Rupiah         9150.00        8988.00       -1.77\par  Rupee            44.28          44.26       -0.05\par  Ringgit         3.5015         3.5270       