\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Calm prevails before U.S. jobs data  \b0\par\par\uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2006\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little moved on Friday, with trade thin as some markets closed for a holiday and dealers waited for U.S. jobs data later in the day for direction.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean markets are shut for the local thanksgiving Chusok holiday, Taiwan is closed for the Mid-Autumn Moon Festival and Chinese markets have been shut all week.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> hit fresh four-month highs around 45.56 per dollar on robust foreign inflows into local stocks.  \par \par \uc2 Most traders said they preferred to stay on the sidelines before the U.S. non-farm payrolls report at 1230 GMT, which could shed some light on the interest rate outlook after data this week stoked speculation that the next U.S. rate move could be a cut.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect the data to show the U.S. economy created 125,000 new jobs in September against 128,000 jobs in August.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a bit of a downside risk to payrolls," said Jimmy Koh, head of economics and treasury research at United Overseas Bank. "The reason for a rally in stocks that we are seeing is that the next Fed move is more likely to be a cut then a hike."  \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> hit a record closing high on Thursday and the S&P 500 index <.SPX> rose to 5-1/2-year highs. Analysts say the U.S. stocks rally bodes well for Asian equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite signs of a U.S. slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 WEAK YEN SHRUGGED OFF  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose almost 0.4 percent to 118.15 yen <JPY=>, heading towards this week's 5-1/2-month peak at about 118.40 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Yen weakness initially weighed on the regionals but by late trade most of them had pared early falls.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> was up slightly at 37.53 per dollar, the Philippine peso moved to the firm side of 50 per dollar <PHP=> and the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was steady at 9,200 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's benchmark interest rate may fall to between 9.0-9.5 percent by year-end from 10.75 percent currently, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Aslim Tadjuddin said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the prospect for further rate cuts should bolster foreign demand for local debt and the rupiah, while traders said that with rate cuts priced into the market the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain in a range.  \par \par \uc2 "Bank Indonesia is doing the right thing in cutting rates as inflation is low," said a Jakarta trader. "When rates reach a bottom, we could see a reaction in the market, but until then there shouldn't be much reaction."  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said they remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail strength in the peso, which hit its highest level in more than four years earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "Because exporters are worried that if the peso strengthens further they will be hurt, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has occasionally been coming into the market," said a Manila dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar hugged a narrow 1.5860-1.5842 range versus the dollar, with focus on Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS, which conducts its monetary policy through the Singapore dollar, is widely expected to maintain a policy allowing a modest and gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "With inflation just 0.7 percent year-on-year in August, there is an outside chance of a (very SGD-negative) switch to a neutral stance on SGD," Westpac senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sean Callow said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that with the Singapore and global economies still firm, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not sound as if it is ready to change its stance.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0808 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.04         117.72       -0.27\par  Sing dlr        1.5838         1.5842       +0.03\par  Baht             37.53          37.59       +0.16\par  Peso             49.92          49.93       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9200.00        9202.00       +0.02\par  Rupee            45.56          45.66       +0.23\par  Ringgit         3.6875         3.6830       -0.12\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.04         117.96       -0.07\par  Sing dlr        1.5838         1.6625       +4.97\par  Baht             37.53          41.11       +9.54\par  Peso             49.92          53.09       +6.35\par  Rupiah         9200.00        9835.00       +6.90\par  Rupee            45.56          45.04       -1.13\par  Ringgit         3.6875         3.7790       +2.48 ((Reporting\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by Dhara Ranasinghe, editing by Jacqueline Wong; Reuters Messaging: dhara.ranasinghe.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65-6870 3840))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click <FXNEWS>  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highl