\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Politics to the fore, rupiah hit.  \b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, March 23 (Reuters) - Local politics, again at\par the fore of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading on Tuesday, weighed on the\par Indonesian rupiah, Philippine peso and Taiwan dollar.\par     Analysts said the recent political and election surprises\par in South Korea and Taiwan had turned investors wary on other\par parts of the region where elections are due -- Indonesia and\par the Philippines.\par     The Singapore dollar        and the Korean won\par   were higher, following the yen        after security\par threats undermined the U.S. dollar.\par     Those concerns arose after Israel killed the spiritual\par leader of the Hamas militant group and as Pakistani troops\par remain locked in battle with a group of suspected al Qaeda\par fighters.\par     Meanwhile, the Indonesian rupiah        continued falling\par in the run-up to parliamentary elections in early April and, at\par 8,590 per dollar, its losses so far this year were over two\par percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Philippine peso        languished near Monday's\par all-time low of 56.45 per dollar because of mounting anxiety\par ahead of the May elections, although it won some reprieve from\par data showing the February fiscal deficit was within the target.\par     "In places where there have been elections they have all\par been surprises, Malaysia on the upside, Taiwan and Korea\par considerably on the downside," said James Malcolm, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist with JP Morgan.\par     Malcolm was referring to the landslide victory for\par Malaysian Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi's ruling\par coalition, the recent impeachment of South Korean President Roh\par Moo-hyun and Taiwan's disputed presidential election.\par  \par      "It makes people nervous about the other risks in Asia,\par particularly Indonesia and the Philippines," Malcolm said.\par     The hangover of President Chen Shui-bian's narrow election\par victory on the weekend preceded by a puzzling assassination\par attempt continued to hurt the Taiwan dollar on Tuesday.\par     It recovered on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 after Chen's Democratic Progressive Party proposed a revision  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the election law to allow a recount of votes if the margin  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of difference was too low.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks have been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Philippines\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Taiwan this week but, as the recent rises  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the won showed, they were probably more tolerant now of  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation, Malcolm said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Central banks will be much be less active, both because of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar/yen and that there's a lot less speculative element in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the market at the moment," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is a more conducive environment for allowing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 appreciation."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0823 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.73         106.93       +0.19\par  Sing dlr        1.6880         1.6923       +0.25\par  Taiwan dlr      33.285         33.285       +0.00\par  Korean won     1156.10        1160.20       +0.35\par  Baht             39.48          39.47       -0.03\par  Peso             56.37          56.42       +0.09\par  Rupiah         8586.00        8560.00       -0.30\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.73         107.31       +0.54\par  Sing dlr        1.6880         1.6978       +0.58\par  Taiwan dlr      33.285         33.978       +2.08\par  Korean won     1156.10        1192.00       +3.11\par  Baht             39.48          39.59       +0.27\par  Peso             56.37          55.49       -1.56\par  Rupiah         8586.00        8410.00       -2.05.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n0017j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
