\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; Peso At New 4-Mo Low \b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2003\par 11:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Philippine peso slid to a fresh four-month low Tuesday, still weighed by a failed mutiny by junior army officers over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weaker following the yen's overnight fall to a one-month low against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was steady as corporate dollar sales offset the impact of the weaker yen, while foreign equity inflows helped lift the New Taiwan dollar, but that rise was tempered by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The effect of the mutiny in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to be felt in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the dollar rising as high as 54.750 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit trimmed gains to end at a fresh four-month closing high of PHP54.670, up from PHP54.380 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't selling dollars to support the weak peso Tuesday. He said the peso's slide will "not significantly" affect the country's inflation outlook. \par \par \uc2  Later in the session, Buenaventura said the peso is expected to recover to PHP53 to the dollar in the fourth quarter of 2002 when export earnings and investments improve. \par \par \uc2  He said the seasonal rise in importers' requirements for dollars and the weaker performance of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have undermined the peso. This was exacerbated by the mutiny on Sunday and also by the escape of suspected Indonesian terrorist Fathur Rohman Al-Ghozi from a Philippine jail July 14. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the peso at PHP55 to the dollar. If left unchecked, the peso could move to "uncharted territory" and that would put pressure on domestic interest rates and affect the government's debt servicing, said Philip Wee, a market strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America said the weekend mutiny "posed only a tiny threat to the government" but "may have reminded the world that political risk in Emerging Southeast Asia may be on the verge of another comeback." \par \par \uc2  "Perception of such risk is already important in differentiating between the markets - however, the risk is for even greater differentiation as 2004 approaches," the bank said in a research note. Both Indonesia and the Philippines will hold presidential polls next year. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar ended against the South Korean won at 1,178.7, barely changed from KRW1,178.8 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Exporters continued to sell dollars, but traders were reluctant to try to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower for fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to support the dollar, market players said. \par \par \uc2  Comments by a key Bank of Korea official early Tuesday that the current dollar/won level isn't hurting exports much had little impact on the market. Traders said the remark didn't give a clear direction for the dollar's move against the won. \par \par \uc2  Against its Taiwan counterpart, the U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.380, down slightly from Monday's NT$34.388. Trading volume totaled US$526 million, up sharply from US$236 million in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Foreign equity inflows, stimulated by a recent relaxation of rules for foreign investors, continued to put pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though not as heavily as it has done in some recent sessions, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net buyers of NT$2.99 billion in shares Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Rising month-end dollar demand from local importers pushed the Indonesian rupiah lower despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local unit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 8,610 rupiah, up from IDR8,530 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The government's official announcement not to renew the multibillion-dollar bailout program with the International Monetary Program next year had little effect on the market, as the decision was widely expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7528 late in Asia, up from S$1.7480 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at 42.01 against the Thai baht, up from THB41.99 previously. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Mary de Wet \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030729dz7t000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
