\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Torn between weak US dlr and weak won. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 7 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stalled on Friday, torn between a falling dollar after U.S. President George W. Bush reinforced market opinion that a conflict in Iraq is imminent and persistent weakness in the South Korean won. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell to a four-year low against the euro after Bush said diplomacy was in its final stages. He said refusing to use force to disarm Iraq would be an unacceptable risk and that he would seek a U.N. resolution authorising such action within days. \par \par \uc2  That helped the Singapore dollar, Thai baht and Taiwan dollar off their lows, but these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dithered as the won stayed near its three-month low of 1,220, which was struck in offshore deals. \par \par \uc2  The yen also hesitated on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and markets were taking few chances ahead of another U.N. inspectors' report on Iraqi compliance later on Friday. \par \par \uc2  David Simmonds, markets strategist with Royal Bank of Scotland, said the sudden offshore fall in the won caught players by surprise, and spurred more won selling. \par \par \uc2  The won was pressured by the risks from North Korea, amplified by Thursday's deployment of U.S. bombers to a Pacific island and news the North is preparing to launch a missile. \par \par \uc2  "It is interesting. Dollar/Asia started off pretty well bid this morning but there hasn't really been a particularly significant follow through," said Simmonds. \par \par \uc2  Some said the pressure on the dollar as war risks mount would be the more dominant factor for regionals. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should be under more pressure. Besides the yen, peso and won, most of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain their strength," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist with BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  SGD LOSES TO BAHT \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar came off a one-week low of 1.7380 per dollar, although analysts said it remained in the weaker half of the undisclosed trade-weighted band authorities manage it in. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht followed, at a more leisurely pace, to 42.51/55 per dollar from around 42.65. \par \par \uc2  The cross rate showed the Singapore dollar was gradually weakening against the baht, having moved to 24.47 baht per Sing dollar from 24.65 in a week, a trend analysts have been anticipating. \par \par \uc2  "The Sing dollar belongs in the weak half of the policy band because of growth concerns. And, it has outperformed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Taiwan dollar and Thai baht in the past 12 months, relative to the dollar," Simmonds said. \par \par \uc2  Simmonds said the similarity of Singapore's and Korea's exports also meant it competed more than others in the region for export competitiveness with the won. Thailand's robust growth and fiscal position were also giving the baht an edge, he added. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR SURPRISES \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar surprised traders by rallying from a low of 34.72, in line with southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than staying weak with the won, defying market belief that the fortunes of the North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were intertwined. \par \par \uc2  The won posted a 13-week closing low in onshore deals, while Korean stocks also extended a three-day losing streak. \par \par \uc2  The popular yen-won cross moved up to near 10.4, the highest since November 2001. \par \par \uc2  "A combination of negatives such as higher oil prices, a weaker economy and greater security risks in the peninsula have shifted market psychology enough for dollar/won to de-couple strongly with dollar/yen," UBS Warburg said in a report. \par \par \uc2  "Though the cross has displayed a strong mean reverting tendency towards 10.0, we believe the rate could go even higher under the current circumstances," it said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso maintained a tight range as it retested the two-year low of 54.85 per dollar it struck on Thursday, with sentiment still negative after Tuesday's deadly bombings in the southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide dollars around 54.85. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it would provide liquidity if markets became too volatile. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, and again on Friday. But it has also been using moral suasion, asking banks to ease their dollar purchases beyond genuine needs. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0845 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.22       117.30         +0.07\par   Sing dlr      1.7355       1.7333         -0.13\par   Taiwan dlr    34.640       34.668         +0.08\par   Korean won   1218.60      1210.20         -0.69\par   Baht           42.53        42.57         +0.11\par   Peso           54.84        54.85         +0.02\par   Rupiah       8880.00      8869.00         -0.12\par   Change on year\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.22     