\par\par \b \uc2  Philippine Stocks, Peso Seen Tumbling Wed On Davao Blasts \b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 23:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MANILA -(Dow Jones)- The Philippine peso and stocks are expected to tumble Wednesday after a bomb explosion in an international airport in Davao, a thriving southern city, aggravated the shaky security situation in the country. \par \par \uc2  A powerful bomb hidden in a backpack killed at least 19 and wounded about 150 people when it ripped through a crowded waiting area just outside the Davao airport on Tuesday. A U.S. missionary was among those killed. \par \par \uc2  Minutes after, a second bomb exploded in Tagum City, an hour's drive from Davao City. \par \par \uc2  "Wednesday will be a very bad day for the peso as players become wary of more violence," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a foreign bank in Manila. \par \par \uc2  The country's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were already closed Tuesday when the bombs exploded, but Philippine bonds immediately underperformed on news of the Davao explosions. \par \par \uc2  The country's EMBI+ widened seven basis points to 555 basis points over Treasurys, while its 2025 global bond lost 7/8 to 100 7/8 bid. \par \par \uc2  The peso recouped some of its recent losses to close at PHP54.500 to the dollar Tuesday as the market heaved a sigh of relief that the country may escape sanctions after legislators pledged to amend the country's anti-money laundering law. \par \par \uc2  Traders believe that the breakthrough in the talks to amend the anti-dirty money law will be offset by the latest bombing incident in the south, which could delay the dollar's pullback. \par \par \uc2  The stock market is also expected to take a beating Wednesday, with the Philippine Stock Exchange Index possibly moving towards its 1010-point immediate support. \par \par \uc2  "The bombing incident will definitely damage the country's image further and erode domestic investors confidence," says AB Capital Securities economist Jose Vistan. "The market will definitely undergo a correction now." \par \par \uc2  The main stock market index closed up 1.44 points, or 0.1%, at 1025.99 Tuesday, bringing its total gain to 2.6% since Feb. 27. \par \par \uc2  Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura refused to project the fallout on the peso of the bombings. "We will have to see what happens," he told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso, Buenaventura replied: "We will see." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers are certain the peso is in for a hammering on worsening security situation. The Davao blasts just came in days after a car bomb exploded in another airport in Mindanao at the height of the fighting between Muslim rebels and the military. \par \par \uc2  "We're talking of a very big city and a vital facility such as an airport. It will definitely create a lot of pressure on the peso," said Roland Avante, treasurer with Chinatrust Commercial Bank (\b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) Inc. \par \par \uc2  Avante said a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should prevent the dollar from hitting key level PHP55. The peso has been lingering at its 25-month low in recent days. \par \par \uc2  The stock market is also expected to suffer. \par \par \uc2  The 30-company Philippine Stock Exchange composite index closed 1.44 points higher Tuesday at 1025.99, adding to the cumulative gain of 24.28 points during the last three days. \par \par \uc2  "Expect the index to test the 1000 support," said Jonathan Ravelas, chief strategist with Banco de Oro Universal Bank. "This is really bad." \par \par \uc2  Other traders believe this development won't trigger a substantial decline because most investors have been on the sidelines anyway. \par \par \uc2  "There will be some selling but I don't think the 1,000 support will be breached. There are very few buyers in the market in the first place," said Eagle Equities president Joseph Roxas. \par \par \uc2  Over the last few months, the local financial market has been battered by a string of bad news including the threats of war in Iraq, a further widening of the government's budget deficit and a possible sanction by the Paris-based Financial Action Task Force against money laundering. \par \par \uc2  -By Ditas Lopez and Cris Larano, Dow Jones Newswires; 63-9185006312; ditas.lopez@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030304dz34001eg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
