\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won Ends Winning Streak \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 962 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2002\par 11:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- -Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hemmed in within tight ranges Wednesday with few leads to spur market players into new positions as the major units, especially the dollar/yen, gave limited guidance. \par \par \uc2  Participants were also reluctant to make any strong bets ahead of the upcoming Thanksgiving holiday in the U.S., starting Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, the Philippine peso and the New Taiwan dollar were slightly weaker; the Singapore dollar and the Thai baht were little changed; while the Indonesian rupiah was modestly higher. \par \par \uc2  "It's been rather rangey. People are not taking strong positions either side (of the dollar versus regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades) as the dollar/yen is not doing much," said a trader at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  There were small amounts of buying but they weren't sufficient for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs to break out of their narrow trading bands, he added. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended a four-day winning streak, hurt by dollar demand from local importers, which helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  erase early losses, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,207.6 won, marginally up from KRW1,206.1 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially headed south, hitting a trough of KRW1,202.5 as market players sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following continued foreign buying of local shares. \par \par \uc2  But importers started buying dollars later in the session, which helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverse track to rise to an intraday high of KRW1,207.9. \par \par \uc2  "The market was directionless Wednesday. Local importers' (won) selling and exporters' (won) buying largely affected the market without any big market-moving factors," said a trader at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the New Taiwan dollar was softer as offshore traders took profits on long positions that they had held for some time, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.820, up from NT$34.773 Tuesday, after trading between NT$34.735 and NT$34.823. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was sharply higher at US$512.5 million, compared with US$285 million in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suspicion that the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso kept many banks wary of further accumulating dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The peso earlier this week sank to a new 16-month low on concerns about the country's swelling budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 53.600 on the Philippine Dealing System, marginally up from PHP53.590 Tuesday, after moving between PHP53.550 and PHP53.600. Trading volume nearly halved to $62.5 million from $113 million in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Several traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably sold around $20 million to $30 million worth of dollars at PHP53.60 Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't immediately be reached for comment. \par \par \uc2  The market is awaiting third-quarter domestic product data, scheduled to be issued at 0200 GMT Thursday. Many economists expect the pace of on-year growth to have slowed to around 3.9% in the third quarter, from 4.5% in the second. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah recovered from initial losses as state banks were suspected to have sold dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended the Asian trading day at IDR8,975, down from IDR8,990 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar rose to an intraday high of IDR9,010 as interbank players covered short dollar positions ahead of the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia was then believed to have sold dollars to cap the U.S. unit's rise, effectively dragging it down below the psychologically-important IDR9,000 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign banks also sold dollars, likely on behalf of investors that were buying assets held by the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency and needed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for payment, they added. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was fairly steady toward the end of the Asian trading day, but gained ground during the European session. \par \par \uc2  As of 1040 GMT the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted around S$1.7652, down from S$1.7672 late Tuesday and S1.7674 near the end of the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  For most of the time, the local dollar was following the yen's gyrations against the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some market participants said the Singapore dollar may be flirting with the higher end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's policy band, within which it guides the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar remained well bid despite U.S. dollar's overnight weakness, perhaps reflecting the wide recognition that SGD (Singapore dollar) is approaching the extremely strong portion of the existing trading band," Bank of America said in a research note Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was hardly changed at THB42.44 at the close of Asian trading hours, from THB43.42 Tuesday. It was trapped in a tight THB43.38-THB43.44 band during the session. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was extremely thin as most offshore players were on the sidelines ahead of key economic data to be announced in Europe and the U.S. overnight, and the U.S, Thanksgiving holiday that starts Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Economic growth projections for the year from the Finance Ministry earlier Wednesday had little impact on the market as they were within the 4.5% to 5% range it had forecast earlier. \par \par \uc2  The ministry said it expects 2002 GDP growth of 4.8%, as a strong domestic economy pushed third-quarter growth to an estimated 5.4% over the year-ago period. \par \par \uc2  GDP growth in the fourth quarter is estimated at 4.8%, said Wisudhi Srisuphan, director-general of the ministry's Fiscal Policy Office. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021127dybr000lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
