\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Arroyo after visiting Mahathir - "I'm like that". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2001\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Philippine President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo wound up a three-day visit to Malaysia in a fighting mood on Thursday, saying she was ready to step up action against speculators, crime syndicates and Abu Sayyaf rebels. \par \par \uc2  With a truce signed with the Moro Islamic Liberation Front (MILF) packed in her bags, along with an agreement with Malaysia to fight piracy in the seas, Arroyo's briefing to reporters at the end of her maiden state visit was full of vim. \par \par \uc2  The petite 55-year-old saw traces of herself in the "plain talk and common-sense" of Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad, the last of a generation of southeast Asian strongmen. \par \par \uc2  "I'm much like that too," said Arroyo, whose eight months in charge of the sometimes unruly Philippine democracy contrasts with Mahathir's 20-year grip on power. \par \par \uc2  There will be no ransom negotiations with the Abu Sayyaf, Arroyo said. "That's ruled out. Negotiating to come into the fold of the law, that's all right," she said. \par \par \uc2  The Abu Sayyaf, which is holding more than 20 hostages, including Americans, is now the only organised group still fighting in the mostly Muslim southern Philippines after the truce signed by the MILF in Kuala Lumpur this week. \par \par \uc2  "With the Abu Sayyaf the idea is to stop giving in," Arroyo said. Earlier this week the Philippine army said it aimed to remove the threat from Abu Sayyaf kidnappers by November. \par \par \uc2  The rebels are holding two American missionaries and claim to have killed a third American. \par \par \uc2  Arroyo said the tough message was also getting through to Manila's crime syndicates as law enforcement agencies were solving crimes faster than they did under the rule of her predecessor, Joseph Estrada. \par \par \uc2  Estrada is currently facing corruption charges and his alleged links to crime syndicates was a factor in his downfall in January. \par \par \uc2  SPECULATORS BEWARE \par \par \uc2  Arroyo also had a warning for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the Philippines might resort to fixing its exchange rate or imposing capital controls, as Mahathir had done at the height of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in 1998, she said: "I can't rule that out. I don't envision it, but I don't rule it out." \par \par \uc2  Arroyo has urged her \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restore the peso to 50 per dollar by the year end and protect its value. \par \par \uc2  But she said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained independent to run policy and she was not giving it orders. \par \par \uc2  The peso was trading around 53 to the dollar on Thursday, and has depreciated by over four percent since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Arroyo said she would be reluctant to impose capital controls, but would keep her options open. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not ruling out anything, a la Mahahtir," Arroyo said, though soon after she spoke Finance Secretary Jose Camacho said the Philippines was committed to free trading of the peso. \par \par \uc2  Arroyo said plans were on track to raise seven billion pesos from privatisations, and that sales of state-owned prime real estate could boost revenues. \par \par \uc2  While admiring Mahathir's handling of the financial crisis three years ago, Arroyo refused to become embroiled in the controversy over the sacking and jailing of Anwar Ibrahim, Mahathir's former deputy and finance minister. \par \par \uc2  Estrada had upset Mahathir by meeting Anwar's wife, Wan Azizah Wan Ismail, but Arroyo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Philippines\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained a policy of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and she would not make the same mistake if Anwar's wife sought a meeting. \par \par \uc2  "Being a guest of the prime minister, if he so much as hinted he would not be happy, I would not got through with it." \par \par \uc2  Arroyo, who came to power following street demonstrations and backing from the military, hinted she aimed to win the presidency through the ballot box next time. \par \par \uc2  "I eligible," she answered when asked if she planned to run in 2004. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010809dx8900b1m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
